add(
  'What is the simple linear regression model and its assumptions?',
  'The SIMPLE LINEAR REGRESSION (SLR) model relates a response Y to a single predictor X via Y_i = beta_0 + beta_1 x_i + epsilon_i, for i = 1, ..., n, where beta_0 (intercept) and beta_1 (slope) are unknown parameters, x_i are FIXED (non-random) known constants, and epsilon_i are random errors. The CLASSICAL ASSUMPTIONS are: (1) LINEARITY — E[epsilon_i | x_i] = 0, so E[Y_i | x_i] = beta_0 + beta_1 x_i (the conditional mean is a straight line in x); (2) HOMOSCEDASTICITY — Var(epsilon_i | x_i) = sigma^2 (constant variance, independent of x); (3) INDEPENDENCE — the epsilon_i are uncorrelated (typically assumed iid, often strengthened to epsilon_i | x_i ~ N(0, sigma^2) for exact inference); (4) EXOGENEITY — the x_i are measured without error and are independent of epsilon (no omitted confounders correlated with x); (5) for inference, NORMALITY — epsilon_i ~ N(0, sigma^2) so Y_i | x_i ~ N(beta_0 + beta_1 x_i, sigma^2). The DESIGN (values x_1, ..., x_n) can be fixed by the experimenter (controlled) or random; in the random-x case, all results are CONDITIONAL on the observed x. The model is called SIMPLE because there is one predictor and LINEAR because it is linear in the parameters beta_0, beta_1 (NOT necessarily linear in x — a model like Y = beta_0 + beta_1 x + beta_2 x^2 + epsilon is linear in parameters but quadratic in x). ESTIMANDS: beta_1 measures the expected change in Y per unit change in x; beta_0 is the expected Y at x = 0 (often extrapolation — center x to make beta_0 interpretable as E[Y | x = xbar]). sigma^2 measures the noise variance around the regression line.',
  'ch08p9_slr_model_assumptions',
  'formula_recall',
  ['simple linear regression', 'SLR', 'linearity', 'homoscedasticity', 'exogeneity', 'normality']
)
add(
  'How is the ordinary least squares (OLS) estimator derived for simple linear regression?',
  'The ORDINARY LEAST SQUARES (OLS) estimator of (beta_0, beta_1) minimizes the RESIDUAL SUM OF SQUARES (RSS): Q(beta_0, beta_1) = sum_{i=1}^{n} (Y_i - beta_0 - beta_1 x_i)^2 = sum e_i^2, where e_i = Y_i - Yhat_i are the residuals. Taking PARTIAL DERIVATIVES and setting them to zero gives the NORMAL EQUATIONS: (1) partial Q/partial beta_0 = -2 sum (Y_i - beta_0 - beta_1 x_i) = 0  =>  sum e_i = 0  =>  Ybar = beta_0_hat + beta_1_hat xbar; (2) partial Q/partial beta_1 = -2 sum x_i (Y_i - beta_0 - beta_1 x_i) = 0  =>  sum x_i e_i = 0  =>  sum x_i Y_i = beta_0_hat sum x_i + beta_1_hat sum x_i^2. SOLVING these two equations yields the OLS estimators: beta_1_hat = sum (x_i - xbar)(Y_i - Ybar) / sum (x_i - xbar)^2 = S_xy / S_xx, and beta_0_hat = Ybar - beta_1_hat xbar, where S_xy = sum (x_i - xbar)(Y_i - Ybar) and S_xx = sum (x_i - xbar)^2. Equivalent form: beta_1_hat = sum (x_i - xbar) Y_i / S_xx (uses the identity sum (x_i - xbar) = 0). GEOMETRIC INTERPRETATION: OLS finds the orthogonal projection of the response vector Y onto the COLUMN SPACE of the design matrix X = [1, x] (the two columns are the constant vector and the x-vector). The residual vector e is orthogonal to both columns (sum e_i = 0 and sum x_i e_i = 0). UNBIASEDNESS: E[beta_1_hat] = beta_1 and E[beta_0_hat] = beta_0 under the SLR assumptions (use E[Y_i] = beta_0 + beta_1 x_i and the linearity of beta_1_hat in Y). VARIANCES: Var(beta_1_hat) = sigma^2 / S_xx (more spread in x => smaller variance); Var(beta_0_hat) = sigma^2 (1/n + xbar^2 / S_xx); Cov(beta_0_hat, beta_1_hat) = -sigma^2 xbar / S_xx (negative if xbar > 0). The fitted value at x* is Yhat(x*) = beta_0_hat + beta_1_hat x* with Var(Yhat(x*)) = sigma^2 (1/n + (x* - xbar)^2 / S_xx) — minimum at x* = xbar, growing as x* moves away (extrapolation is uncertain).',
  'ch08p9_slr_ols_derivation',
  'formula_recall',
  ['OLS', 'ordinary least squares', 'normal equations', 'residual sum of squares', 'projection']
)
add(
  'What are the fitted values, residuals, and the residual sum of squares in simple linear regression?',
  'In SLR, the FITTED VALUES (predicted values) are Yhat_i = beta_0_hat + beta_1_hat x_i = Ybar + beta_1_hat (x_i - xbar), for i = 1, ..., n. The vector of fitted values Yhat = X beta_hat = H Y, where H = X (X^T X)^{-1} X^T is the HAT MATRIX (in SLR, H is n x n and projects Y onto the column space of X). The RESIDUALS are e_i = Y_i - Yhat_i, with the property sum e_i = 0 (from the normal equation for beta_0) and sum x_i e_i = 0 (from the normal equation for beta_1). The RESIDUAL SUM OF SQUARES (RSS), also called SUM OF SQUARED ERRORS (SSE) or SUM OF SQUARED RESIDUALS, is RSS = sum_{i=1}^{n} e_i^2 = sum (Y_i - Yhat_i)^2. The MEAN SQUARED ERROR (MSE) is s^2 = RSS / (n - 2), which is an UNBIASED estimator of sigma^2 (the divisor n - 2 reflects 2 parameters estimated: beta_0 and beta_1). The residual STANDARD ERROR is s = sqrt(s^2). DEGREES OF FREEDOM: total = n - 1 (after subtracting Ybar); regression = 1 (one slope, in SLR); residual/error = n - 2. ANOVA IDENTITY: SST = SSR + SSE, where SST = sum (Y_i - Ybar)^2 (total sum of squares), SSR = sum (Yhat_i - Ybar)^2 (regression sum of squares), and SSE = RSS. The COEFFICIENT OF DETERMINATION is R^2 = SSR / SST = 1 - SSE / SST, taking values in [0, 1]; R^2 measures the fraction of variance in Y explained by the linear regression on x. In SLR (one predictor), R^2 = (sample correlation r)^2 — i.e., R^2 is the squared Pearson correlation between x and Y. ADJUSTED R^2 (more relevant in multiple regression) is R^2_adj = 1 - (SSE / (n - p)) / (SST / (n - 1)) where p is the number of parameters. STANDARDIZED RESIDUALS r_i = e_i / (s sqrt(1 - h_ii)) are useful for diagnostics (see leverage item). The PRESS STATISTIC (Predicted Residual Sum of Squares) is sum (e_{(i)})^2 where e_{(i)} = Y_i - Yhat_{(i)} is the LEAVE-ONE-OUT prediction residual; PRESS = sum (e_i / (1 - h_ii))^2 is computable from a single fit (see leverage item).',
  'ch08p9_slr_fitted_residuals_rss',
  'formula_recall',
  ['fitted values', 'residuals', 'RSS', 'SSE', 'hat matrix', 'R-squared', 'PRESS']
)
add(
  'How is the ANOVA decomposition derived for simple linear regression?',
  'In SLR, the ANALYSIS OF VARIANCE (ANOVA) DECOMPOSITION partitions the total variability in Y into explained and unexplained components: SST = SSR + SSE, where SST = sum_{i=1}^{n} (Y_i - Ybar)^2 (TOTAL SUM OF SQUARES), SSR = sum (Yhat_i - Ybar)^2 (REGRESSION SUM OF SQUARES, the variability explained by the model), and SSE = sum (Y_i - Yhat_i)^2 = RSS (ERROR SUM OF SQUARES). DERIVATION of the identity: SST = sum (Y_i - Ybar)^2 = sum [(Yhat_i - Ybar) + (Y_i - Yhat_i)]^2 = sum (Yhat_i - Ybar)^2 + sum (Y_i - Yhat_i)^2 + 2 sum (Yhat_i - Ybar)(Y_i - Yhat_i). The cross-product term vanishes: sum (Yhat_i - Ybar)(Y_i - Yhat_i) = sum (Yhat_i - Ybar) e_i = sum Yhat_i e_i - Ybar sum e_i = 0 - 0 = 0, because (a) sum e_i = 0 and (b) sum Yhat_i e_i = sum (beta_0_hat + beta_1_hat x_i) e_i = beta_0_hat sum e_i + beta_1_hat sum x_i e_i = 0 (using both normal equations). DEGREES OF FREEDOM: SST has n - 1 (one mean estimated), SSR has 1 (one slope; in SLR), SSE has n - 2 (two parameters estimated: intercept and slope). MEAN SQUARES: MSR = SSR / 1 = SSR, MSE = SSE / (n - 2) (unbiased for sigma^2). F-STATISTIC for overall significance: F = MSR / MSE = (SSR / 1) / (SSE / (n - 2)), which under H_0: beta_1 = 0 has an F distribution with (1, n - 2) degrees of freedom. In SLR, this F-statistic equals the SQUARE of the t-statistic for beta_1: F = t^2 (since F_{1, n-2} = t^2_{n-2}). COEFFICIENT OF DETERMINATION: R^2 = SSR / SST = 1 - SSE / SST. RELATIONSHIP TO CORRELATION: in SLR, R^2 = r^2 where r = S_xy / sqrt(S_xx S_yy) is the sample Pearson correlation; SSR = beta_1_hat S_xy = S_xy^2 / S_xx. EXPECTED MEAN SQUARES (under the full model with normality): E[MSE] = sigma^2; E[MSR] = sigma^2 + beta_1^2 S_xx (so MSR > MSE on average when beta_1 != 0). The ANOVA table is the foundation for the overall F-test, R^2, and inference on beta_1.',
  'ch08p9_slr_anova_decomposition',
  'formula_recall',
  ['ANOVA decomposition', 'SST', 'SSR', 'SSE', 'F-statistic', 'R-squared', 'degrees of freedom']
)
add(
  'What is the inference for the slope and intercept in simple linear regression?',
  'Under the SLR assumptions WITH NORMALITY (epsilon_i ~ N(0, sigma^2) iid), the OLS estimators are normally distributed: beta_1_hat ~ N(beta_1, sigma^2 / S_xx) and beta_0_hat ~ N(beta_0, sigma^2 (1/n + xbar^2 / S_xx)), independently of s^2 = SSE / (n - 2). The t-STATISTICS are: T_1 = (beta_1_hat - beta_1) / (s / sqrt(S_xx)) ~ t_{n-2} and T_0 = (beta_0_hat - beta_0) / (s sqrt(1/n + xbar^2/S_xx)) ~ t_{n-2}, where SE(beta_1_hat) = s / sqrt(S_xx) and SE(beta_0_hat) = s sqrt(1/n + xbar^2/S_xx) are the STANDARD ERRORS. HYPOTHESIS TEST for the slope: H_0: beta_1 = beta_1^{(0)} vs H_a: beta_1 != beta_1^{(0)} (two-sided) or one-sided. The test statistic T = (beta_1_hat - beta_1^{(0)}) / SE(beta_1_hat) ~ t_{n-2} under H_0. The most common test is H_0: beta_1 = 0 (no linear relationship) vs H_a: beta_1 != 0; reject if |T| > t_{n-2, 1-alpha/2}. The p-value is 2 P(T_{n-2} > |T_obs|). Note this is EQUIVALENT to the overall F-test in SLR (F = T^2). CONFIDENCE INTERVALS: 100(1-alpha)% CI for beta_1 is beta_1_hat +/- t_{n-2, 1-alpha/2} * SE(beta_1_hat) = beta_1_hat +/- t_{n-2, 1-alpha/2} * s / sqrt(S_xx). Similarly for beta_0. JOINT CONFIDENCE REGION for (beta_0, beta_1): the 100(1-alpha)% joint region is the ELLIPSE {(beta_0, beta_1) : ((beta_hat - beta)^T X^T X (beta_hat - beta) / 2) / s^2 <= F_{2, n-2, 1-alpha}}, centered at beta_hat with shape determined by X^T X (the ellipse is more elongated along the direction of higher uncertainty; covariance between beta_0_hat and beta_1_hat is reflected in the tilt of the ellipse). PREDICTION AT x*: Yhat(x*) = beta_0_hat + beta_1_hat x*. TWO TYPES OF INTERVALS at x*: (1) CONFIDENCE INTERVAL for the MEAN RESPONSE E[Y | x*] = beta_0 + beta_1 x*: Yhat(x*) +/- t_{n-2, 1-alpha/2} * s * sqrt(1/n + (x* - xbar)^2 / S_xx); (2) PREDICTION INTERVAL for a NEW INDIVIDUAL observation Y_0 = beta_0 + beta_1 x* + epsilon_0: Yhat(x*) +/- t_{n-2, 1-alpha/2} * s * sqrt(1 + 1/n + (x* - xbar)^2 / S_xx). The PI is WIDER (has the extra "1 +" for the variance of the new error epsilon_0). Both intervals are narrowest at x* = xbar and widen as x* moves away (the BOW-TIE / HOURGLASS shape).',
  'ch08p9_slr_inference_slope_intercept',
  'formula_recall',
  ['t-test', 'slope', 'intercept', 'standard error', 'confidence interval', 'prediction interval', 'mean response']
)
add(
  'What is the prediction interval for a new observation in simple linear regression?',
  'In SLR, suppose we want to predict a NEW observation Y_0 at a future value x_0 of the predictor. The model is Y_0 = beta_0 + beta_1 x_0 + epsilon_0, where epsilon_0 ~ N(0, sigma^2) is INDEPENDENT of the original sample (and hence of beta_0_hat, beta_1_hat, s^2). The POINT PREDICTOR is Yhat_0 = beta_0_hat + beta_1_hat x_0, which is unbiased: E[Yhat_0] = E[Y_0] = beta_0 + beta_1 x_0. The PREDICTION ERROR is Y_0 - Yhat_0 = (beta_0 + beta_1 x_0 + epsilon_0) - (beta_0_hat + beta_1_hat x_0) = (beta_0 - beta_0_hat) + x_0 (beta_1 - beta_1_hat) + epsilon_0. The VARIANCE of the prediction error has TWO components: (1) the variance from estimating beta_0 and beta_1, Var(Yhat_0) = sigma^2 (1/n + (x_0 - xbar)^2 / S_xx); (2) the variance of the new error, Var(epsilon_0) = sigma^2. By independence, Var(Y_0 - Yhat_0) = sigma^2 (1 + 1/n + (x_0 - xbar)^2 / S_xx). Substituting s for sigma gives the STANDARD ERROR OF PREDICTION: SE_pred = s sqrt(1 + 1/n + (x_0 - xbar)^2 / S_xx). The 100(1-alpha)% PREDICTION INTERVAL (PI) for Y_0 is: Yhat_0 +/- t_{n-2, 1-alpha/2} * s * sqrt(1 + 1/n + (x_0 - xbar)^2 / S_xx). CONTRAST WITH CI FOR THE MEAN RESPONSE: the CI for E[Y | x_0] = beta_0 + beta_1 x_0 uses SE_mean = s sqrt(1/n + (x_0 - xbar)^2 / S_xx) (without the "1 +"). Hence the PI is always WIDER than the CI for the mean at the same x_0, by the extra term sigma^2 from epsilon_0. As n -> infinity, the CI for the mean shrinks to a point (estimation error -> 0), but the PI approaches Yhat_0 +/- z_{1-alpha/2} * sigma (we cannot eliminate the irreducible noise epsilon_0). SHAPE: both intervals are narrowest at x_0 = xbar and widen as x_0 moves away from xbar (the "bow-tie" / "hourglass" shape), reflecting greater uncertainty in extrapolation. EXTRAPOLATION WARNING: PIs at x_0 outside the range of the observed x_i are unreliable — the linear model may not hold beyond the data range. SIMULTANEOUS PREDICTION INTERVALS (Scheffe or Bonferroni) are needed for predicting k future observations with overall coverage 1-alpha.',
  'ch08p9_slr_prediction_interval',
  'formula_recall',
  ['prediction interval', 'new observation', 'prediction error', 'bow-tie shape', 'extrapolation']
)
add(
  'What is regression through the origin and when is it used?',
  'REGRESSION THROUGH THE ORIGIN (RTO) is the special case of SLR where the intercept is constrained to be ZERO: Y_i = beta_1 x_i + epsilon_i, i = 1, ..., n, with the same error assumptions (zero mean, constant variance, independence, optional normality). The OLS estimator minimizes Q(beta_1) = sum (Y_i - beta_1 x_i)^2; setting dQ/d beta_1 = -2 sum x_i (Y_i - beta_1 x_i) = 0 gives the NORMAL EQUATION sum x_i Y_i = beta_1 sum x_i^2, hence beta_1_hat = sum x_i Y_i / sum x_i^2. PROPERTIES: (1) UNBIASED: E[beta_1_hat] = beta_1 (use E[Y_i] = beta_1 x_i); (2) Var(beta_1_hat) = sigma^2 / sum x_i^2; (3) sigma^2 is estimated by s^2 = sum (Y_i - beta_1_hat x_i)^2 / (n - 1) — note the divisor is n - 1 (only ONE parameter estimated, not two); (4) the residuals e_i = Y_i - beta_1_hat x_i do NOT necessarily sum to zero (unlike the intercept model); (5) the sum of x_i e_i = 0 (still satisfies the single normal equation). WHEN TO USE: (a) when theoretical/physical considerations REQUIRE E[Y | x = 0] = 0 (e.g., Ohm\'s law V = IR with no voltage at zero current; Hooke\'s law with no extension at zero force); (b) when the intercept is statistically and substantively negligible. CAVEATS: RTO can give WORSE fits than the intercept model even when the true intercept is zero, because the residuals are not centered; R^2 computed in the usual way (1 - SSE/SST) can be NEGATIVE because the model may explain less than a horizontal line at Ybar. The R^2 definition must be MODIFIED for RTO (e.g., 1 - SSE / sum Y_i^2 — uncentered). HYPOTHESIS TEST for H_0: beta_0 = 0 (whether to use RTO): fit the full intercept model and test the intercept with a t-test; if not rejected, RTO may be appropriate (but small sample and low power are concerns). DIAGNOSTIC: always plot the data — if the data clearly pass through (0, 0) and the fitted RTO line looks reasonable, use RTO; if the intercept-model fit has a confidence interval for beta_0 containing 0 and small magnitude, RTO is justified; otherwise, use the full intercept model.',
  'ch08p9_regression_through_origin',
  'formula_recall',
  ['regression through origin', 'RTO', 'no intercept', 'normal equation', 'Ohm law']
)
add(
  'How do you estimate sigma^2 in simple linear regression and what is its distribution?',
  'In SLR, the ERROR VARIANCE sigma^2 = Var(epsilon_i) is unknown and must be estimated from the data. The UNBIASED ESTIMATOR is s^2 = SSE / (n - 2) = sum_{i=1}^{n} (Y_i - Yhat_i)^2 / (n - 2), where SSE = RSS is the residual sum of squares and n - 2 is the ERROR DEGREES OF FREEDOM (n observations minus 2 estimated parameters: beta_0 and beta_1). DERIVATION of unbiasedness: under the SLR assumptions (with or without normality), E[SSE] = (n - 2) sigma^2. Sketch: SSE = sum (Y_i - Yhat_i)^2 = sum [(Y_i - mu_i) - (Yhat_i - mu_i)]^2 where mu_i = beta_0 + beta_1 x_i. Writing in matrix form, SSE = Y^T (I - H) Y where H is the hat matrix, and E[Y^T (I - H) Y] = trace((I - H) sigma^2 I) + 0 = sigma^2 (n - trace(H)) = sigma^2 (n - 2) (since trace(H) = rank(H) = 2 in SLR). MAXIMUM LIKELIHOOD ESTIMATOR (under normality): sigma^2_MLE = SSE / n (BIASED downward — divides by n instead of n - 2; MLE is biased because it does not account for the 2 estimated parameters). The factor (n - 2) / n = 1 - 2/n corrects MLE to s^2. DISTRIBUTION under normality: (n - 2) s^2 / sigma^2 = SSE / sigma^2 ~ chi^2_{n-2}, INDEPENDENT of beta_0_hat and beta_1_hat (Cochran\'s theorem). This independence is what makes the t-statistics (with s in the denominator) have exact t_{n-2} distributions. The sample standard deviation s = sqrt(s^2) is the RESIDUAL STANDARD ERROR. CONFIDENCE INTERVAL for sigma^2: [SSE / chi^2_{n-2, 1-alpha/2}, SSE / chi^2_{n-2, alpha/2}] (chi-squared interval, analogous to the single-sample normal-variance CI). Asymptotically (large n), s^2 ~ N(sigma^2, 2 sigma^4 / (n - 2)) (using Var(s^2) = 2 sigma^4 / (n - 2) for normal errors), giving the approximate CI s^2 +/- z_{1-alpha/2} s^2 sqrt(2/(n - 2)). ROBUSTNESS: s^2 is sensitive to outliers (squared residuals amplify large errors); use robust variance estimators (e.g., Huber-White sandwich) if heteroscedasticity is suspected. THE LINK TO INFERENCE: s appears in EVERY standard error in regression (SE(beta_1_hat) = s/sqrt(S_xx), SE(beta_0_hat) = s sqrt(1/n + xbar^2/S_xx), SE for prediction), so accurate estimation of sigma^2 is essential for valid t-tests, F-tests, and confidence intervals.',
  'ch08p9_slr_sigma_squared_estimation',
  'formula_recall',
  ['sigma squared', 'error variance', 'MSE', 'unbiased estimator', 'chi-squared', 'degrees of freedom']
)

