// SECTION 2 — MULTIPLE LINEAR REGRESSION (MATRIX FORM) (7 items)
// ============================================================
add(
  'What is the matrix formulation of the multiple linear regression model?',
  'The MULTIPLE LINEAR REGRESSION (MLR) model with p predictors (and an intercept) for n observations is written in MATRIX FORM as Y = X beta + epsilon, where: (1) Y in R^n is the RESPONSE VECTOR (n x 1); (2) X is the DESIGN MATRIX (n x (p+1)), with row i = (1, x_{i1}, x_{i2}, ..., x_{ip}) — the first column of 1s corresponds to the intercept; (3) beta = (beta_0, beta_1, ..., beta_p)^T in R^{p+1} is the COEFFICIENT VECTOR; (4) epsilon in R^n is the ERROR VECTOR. The CLASSICAL ASSUMPTIONS in matrix form: (A1) LINEARITY — E[epsilon | X] = 0, i.e., E[Y | X] = X beta; (A2) FULL RANK — X has rank p+1 (no perfect multicollinearity; columns are linearly independent); (A3) SPHERICAL ERRORS — Var(epsilon | X) = sigma^2 I_n (homoscedastic and uncorrelated); (A4) NORMALITY (for exact inference) — epsilon | X ~ N(0, sigma^2 I_n). Equivalently, Y | X ~ N(X beta, sigma^2 I_n). The DESIGN MATRIX X is treated as FIXED (conditional analysis) in the classical model; if X is random, all results hold CONDITIONAL on the observed X. The columns of X represent the REGRESSORS (predictors, including the column of 1s for the intercept). INTERPRETATION of beta_j: the expected change in Y for a one-unit increase in x_j, HOLDING ALL OTHER predictors CONSTANT (the "ceteris paribus" interpretation). This is fundamentally different from SLR, where beta_1 is the marginal change ignoring other variables. The "holding constant" interpretation is what makes MLR useful for CONTROLLED experiments and for disentangling the effects of correlated predictors. The GEOMETRY: Y is a vector in R^n; the column space of X (span of its p+1 columns) is a (p+1)-dimensional subspace of R^n; the OLS fit projects Y onto this subspace. POLYNOMIAL regression (e.g., Y = beta_0 + beta_1 x + beta_2 x^2) and INTERACTION models (e.g., Y = beta_0 + beta_1 x_1 + beta_2 x_2 + beta_3 x_1 x_2) are SPECIAL CASES of MLR — the "design matrix columns" include x, x^2, x_1 x_2 as derived regressors. DUMMY VARIABLES for categorical predictors: a k-level factor uses k-1 dummy columns (with one level as the reference); the coefficients represent contrasts with the reference level.',
  'ch08p9_mlr_matrix_formulation',
  'formula_recall',
  ['multiple linear regression', 'MLR', 'matrix form', 'design matrix', 'full rank', 'spherical errors']
)
add(
  'What are the normal equations and the OLS estimator in matrix form?',
  'In MLR (Y = X beta + epsilon), the OLS estimator beta_hat minimizes the RESIDUAL SUM OF SQUARES Q(beta) = (Y - X beta)^T (Y - X beta) = Y^T Y - 2 beta^T X^T Y + beta^T X^T X beta. Taking the GRADIENT with respect to beta and setting it to zero gives the NORMAL EQUATIONS: X^T X beta_hat = X^T Y. Assuming X has FULL COLUMN RANK (rank p+1, no perfect multicollinearity), X^T X is INVERTIBLE, and the OLS ESTIMATOR is: beta_hat = (X^T X)^{-1} X^T Y. PROPERTIES: (1) LINEARITY in Y — beta_hat = A Y where A = (X^T X)^{-1} X^T is a (p+1) x n matrix (depends only on X, not Y); (2) UNBIASEDNESS — E[beta_hat | X] = (X^T X)^{-1} X^T E[Y | X] = (X^T X)^{-1} X^T X beta = beta (uses linearity and E[epsilon | X] = 0); (3) COVARIANCE MATRIX — Var(beta_hat | X) = sigma^2 (X^T X)^{-1} (under homoscedasticity); derivation uses Var(AY) = A Var(Y) A^T = A (sigma^2 I) A^T = sigma^2 A A^T = sigma^2 (X^T X)^{-1} X^T X (X^T X)^{-1} = sigma^2 (X^T X)^{-1}; (4) FITTED VALUES Yhat = X beta_hat = X (X^T X)^{-1} X^T Y = H Y where H = X (X^T X)^{-1} X^T is the HAT MATRIX (projection onto column space of X); (5) RESIDUALS e = Y - Yhat = (I - H) Y, which are orthogonal to the columns of X (X^T e = X^T (I - H) Y = (X^T - X^T H) Y = 0 because X^T H = X^T X (X^T X)^{-1} X^T = X^T); (6) UNBIASED sigma^2 estimator: s^2 = e^T e / (n - p - 1) = SSE / (n - p - 1) where n - p - 1 is the ERROR DEGREES OF FREEDOM (number of observations minus number of estimated parameters); (7) under normality, beta_hat | X ~ N_{p+1}(beta, sigma^2 (X^T X)^{-1}) and (n - p - 1) s^2 / sigma^2 = SSE / sigma^2 ~ chi^2_{n-p-1}, INDEPENDENT of beta_hat (Cochran). NON-INVERTIBLE X^T X: if X is rank-deficient (perfect multicollinearity), (X^T X) is singular; use the MOORE-PENROSE PSEUDOINVERSE (X^T X)^+ (or equivalently the SVD-based solution) to obtain a non-unique minimum-norm solution; in practice, drop the collinear columns or use regularization (ridge). The condition number of X^T X affects NUMERICAL STABILITY — use QR decomposition (preferred) or SVD rather than explicitly forming (X^T X)^{-1}.',
  'ch08p9_mlr_normal_equations_ols',
  'formula_recall',
  ['normal equations', 'OLS matrix form', 'X transpose X', 'hat matrix', 'full rank', 'pseudoinverse']
)
add(
  'What is the hat matrix and what are its key properties?',
  'The HAT MATRIX in MLR is H = X (X^T X)^{-1} X^T, an n x n matrix that maps the response vector Y to the vector of fitted values: Yhat = H Y. The name comes from the fact that H "puts the hat on Y" (Y -> Yhat). PROPERTIES: (1) SYMMETRIC — H = H^T (since (X^T X)^{-1} is symmetric); (2) IDEMPOTENT — H^2 = H X (X^T X)^{-1} X^T H = X (X^T X)^{-1} (X^T X) (X^T X)^{-1} X^T = X (X^T X)^{-1} X^T = H (H is a projection matrix); (3) H projects orthogonally onto the COLUMN SPACE of X (a (p+1)-dimensional subspace of R^n); (4) RANK = trace(H) = p + 1 (the number of parameters); (5) the COMPLEMENT M = I - H is also symmetric and idempotent, with rank n - p - 1, and projects onto the orthogonal complement of col(X); (6) e = Y - Yhat = (I - H) Y = M Y; (7) H X = X (projecting the columns of X onto themselves gives them back) and X^T e = X^T (I - H) Y = 0 (residuals orthogonal to columns of X). LEVERAGE: the DIAGONAL ENTRIES h_ii of H are called LEVERAGES, measuring how much observation i influences its own fitted value: Yhat_i = sum_j h_{ij} Y_j, so h_{ii} = partial Yhat_i / partial Y_i. PROPERTIES of leverages: (a) 0 <= h_ii <= 1 (in SLR h_ii = 1/n + (x_i - xbar)^2 / S_xx, summing to 2/n... no, to 1 for each i is wrong — let me restate: 1/n <= h_ii <= 1 in SLR, and 0 <= h_ii <= 1 in MLR generally); (b) sum_{i=1}^{n} h_{ii} = trace(H) = p + 1 (the average leverage is (p+1)/n); (c) a HIGH-LEVERAGE point has h_ii > 2(p+1)/n (rule of thumb) — it lies far from the centroid of the predictor space (extreme x values); (d) leverage depends ONLY on X (not Y) — an observation can have high leverage without being an outlier (an "influential" observation is one with BOTH high leverage and a large residual). RESIDUAL VARIANCE: Var(e_i) = sigma^2 (1 - h_ii) (since Var(e) = sigma^2 (I - H)), so high-leverage points have LOWER residual variance — they "fit themselves" well (their fitted value is close to their observed Y, so the residual is small on average). STUDENTIZED RESIDUALS r_i = e_i / (s sqrt(1 - h_ii)) standardize for this — they are more useful than raw residuals for detecting outliers. LEAVE-ONE-OUT: e_{(i)} = Y_i - Yhat_{(i)} = e_i / (1 - h_ii) (the residual that observation i would have if the model were fit WITHOUT it, computed from a single full-data fit).',
  'ch08p9_hat_matrix_properties',
  'formula_recall',
  ['hat matrix', 'projection matrix', 'idempotent', 'leverage', 'diagonal', 'leave-one-out']
)
add(
  'How are R-squared and adjusted R-squared defined for multiple linear regression?',
  'In MLR, the COEFFICIENT OF DETERMINATION is R^2 = SSR / SST = 1 - SSE / SST, where SST = sum (Y_i - Ybar)^2 (total, df = n - 1), SSR = sum (Yhat_i - Ybar)^2 (regression, df = p), and SSE = sum (Y_i - Yhat_i)^2 (error, df = n - p - 1). PROPERTIES of R^2: (1) 0 <= R^2 <= 1; (2) R^2 = (sample multiple correlation between Y and Yhat)^2; (3) R^2 NEVER DECREASES when a predictor is ADDED to the model (because OLS minimizes SSE, adding a column to X can only decrease or leave unchanged SSE) — this is the fundamental flaw of using R^2 for model SELECTION: it always favors the largest model; (4) R^2 = 0 corresponds to the "intercept-only" model (Yhat_i = Ybar for all i); (5) R^2 = 1 means perfect fit (SSE = 0). ADJUSTED R^2 (R^2_adj) PENALIZES for the number of predictors: R^2_adj = 1 - (SSE / (n - p - 1)) / (SST / (n - 1)) = 1 - (1 - R^2) (n - 1) / (n - p - 1). PROPERTIES: (1) R^2_adj <= R^2 (always, with equality only when p = 0); (2) R^2_adj CAN DECREASE when a predictor is added — specifically, adding a predictor DECREASES R^2_adj iff the t-statistic for that predictor has |t| < 1 (i.e., the predictor\'s contribution to SSE is less than what would be expected by adding 1 df of error); (3) R^2_adj can be NEGATIVE if the model fits worse than a horizontal line (when SSE / (n - p - 1) > SST / (n - 1), i.e., when p is large relative to n and R^2 is small); (4) R^2_adj is UNBIASED for the population R^2 (under normality) — E[R^2_adj] approximately equals the true population R^2 (the unadjusted R^2 is biased upward). WHEN TO USE: R^2_adj is preferred for COMPARING models with different numbers of predictors (e.g., in stepwise selection); R^2 is fine for reporting the fit of a single pre-specified model. OTHER R^2 GENERALIZATIONS: PSEUDO R^2 in GLMs (McFadden, Nagelkerke, Cox-Snell) for non-Gaussian responses; R^2 in MIXED MODELS (conditional vs marginal R^2, Nakagawa-Schielzeth). PITFALL: a high R^2 does NOT imply causation, does NOT imply the model is correct (omitted variable bias can give high R^2 with biased coefficients), and does NOT imply the predictors are individually significant. AIC, BIC, and cross-validated prediction error are often better criteria for model SELECTION than R^2 or R^2_adj.',
  'ch08p9_mlr_r_squared_adjusted',
  'formula_recall',
  ['R-squared', 'adjusted R-squared', 'model selection', 'multiple correlation', 'penalty']
)
add(
  'What is multicollinearity and how is the variance inflation factor (VIF) defined?',
  'MULTICOLLINEARITY refers to the situation in MLR where two or more predictors are HIGHLY (linearly) CORRELATED with each other, making it difficult to disentangle their individual effects on Y. SYMPTOMS: (1) large standard errors on individual coefficients (low t-statistics) even though the overall F-test is highly significant; (2) coefficients that are sensitive to small changes in the data (adding/dropping an observation changes estimates dramatically); (3) coefficients with the "wrong" sign (e.g., a positive relationship shows a negative coefficient); (4) high pairwise correlations among predictors (visible in a correlation matrix). PERFECT multicollinearity (X has rank < p+1) makes (X^T X) singular and beta_hat non-unique; NEAR multicollinearity (X^T X nearly singular, high condition number) makes (X^T X)^{-1} large, inflating Var(beta_hat) = sigma^2 (X^T X)^{-1}. The VARIANCE INFLATION FACTOR (VIF) quantifies how much the variance of beta_j_hat is inflated by the correlation among predictors. DEFINITION: VIF_j = 1 / (1 - R_j^2), where R_j^2 is the coefficient of determination from regressing x_j on ALL OTHER predictors (x_1, ..., x_{j-1}, x_{j+1}, ..., x_p). DERIVATION: Var(beta_j_hat) = sigma^2 [(X^T X)^{-1}]_{jj}; if x_j were ORTHOGONAL to all other predictors, this would be sigma^2 / S_{x_j x_j} where S_{x_j x_j} = sum (x_{ij} - xbar_j)^2. With correlation, [(X^T X)^{-1}]_{jj} = (1 / S_{x_j x_j}) * (1 / (1 - R_j^2)) = (1 / S_{x_j x_j}) * VIF_j, so Var(beta_j_hat) = sigma^2 VIF_j / S_{x_j x_j}. INTERPRETATION: VIF_j >= 1, with VIF_j = 1 meaning x_j is uncorrelated with other predictors; VIF_j = 5 means the variance is 5x larger than it would be without multicollinearity (equivalently, SE is sqrt(5) ~= 2.24x larger). RULES OF THUMB: VIF > 5 (some say VIF > 10) indicates problematic multicollinearity. REMEDIES: (1) DROP one of the collinear predictors (often the most theoretically justified choice); (2) COMBINE collinear predictors into an index (e.g., PCA, principal components regression); (3) RIDGE REGRESSION (L2 penalty) — biases coefficients but reduces variance; (4) COLLECT MORE DATA (more observations can stabilize the estimates); (5) CENTER predictors (reduces multicollinearity with the intercept, but does not help between predictors); (6) RESPECIFY the model (e.g., use ratios or differences instead of levels). CONDITION NUMBER: kappa(X) = sqrt(lambda_max / lambda_min) (square root of ratio of largest to smallest eigenvalue of X^T X); kappa > 30 indicates serious multicollinearity.',
  'ch08p9_multicollinearity_vif',
  'formula_recall',
  ['multicollinearity', 'VIF', 'variance inflation factor', 'condition number', 'remedies']
)
add(
  'How do you handle categorical predictors and interactions in multiple linear regression?',
  'CATEGORICAL PREDICTORS are encoded using DUMMY VARIABLES (indicator variables). For a categorical predictor with k LEVELS (categories), create k - 1 dummy variables D_1, ..., D_{k-1}, with one level designated as the REFERENCE (baseline) and D_j = 1 if observation is in level j, 0 otherwise. The MLR model becomes Y = beta_0 + beta_1 D_1 + ... + beta_{k-1} D_{k-1} + ... + epsilon. INTERPRETATION: beta_0 is the mean response for the REFERENCE level (all D_j = 0); beta_j is the DIFFERENCE in mean response between level j and the reference level (a contrast). For example, with a 3-level treatment (Control, Low, High), set Control as reference: D_1 = 1 if Low, D_2 = 1 if High; then beta_0 = mean(Control), beta_1 = mean(Low) - mean(Control), beta_2 = mean(High) - mean(Control). HYPOTHESIS TEST for the categorical predictor overall: H_0: beta_1 = beta_2 = ... = beta_{k-1} = 0 (no difference across levels) — tested with a PARTIAL F-TEST (compare the full model to the model without the dummies). ALTERNATIVE ENCODINGS: (a) EFFECT CODING (D_j in {-1, 0, 1}, with the reference coded as all -1s) — beta_0 becomes the GRAND MEAN and beta_j the deviation of level j from the grand mean; (b) HELMERT, FORWARD/BACKWARD DIFFERENCE, POLYNOMIAL contrasts — useful for ordered factors. INTERACTIONS between two predictors x_1 and x_2 are modeled by including the PRODUCT x_1 * x_2 as an additional regressor: Y = beta_0 + beta_1 x_1 + beta_2 x_2 + beta_3 (x_1 x_2) + epsilon. INTERPRETATION: beta_1 is the effect of x_1 when x_2 = 0 (not a "main effect" — it is specific to x_2 = 0); the effect of x_1 at a general x_2 is beta_1 + beta_3 x_2 (so the slope of x_1 changes linearly with x_2); beta_3 is the change in the slope of x_1 per unit increase in x_2. CENTERING predictors before forming interactions makes beta_1 and beta_2 interpretable as effects at the MEAN of the other predictor (and reduces multicollinearity between main effects and the interaction). HIERARCHICAL PRINCIPLE: if an interaction is included, the main effects MUST also be included (even if not significant) — otherwise the interpretation of the interaction is arbitrary. INTERACTION between a continuous x and a categorical D (k levels): include (k-1) product terms (x * D_j) — the slope of x differs across the levels of the categorical. INTERACTION between two categoricals (k_1 and k_2 levels): include (k_1 - 1)(k_2 - 1) product dummies — this is the structure of TWO-WAY ANOVA with interaction.',
  'ch08p9_categorical_predictors_interactions',
  'formula_recall',
  ['categorical predictor', 'dummy variable', 'reference level', 'interaction', 'effect coding', 'hierarchical principle']
)
add(
  'How are fitted values and prediction intervals computed in multiple linear regression?',
  'In MLR, the FITTED VALUES are Yhat = X beta_hat = H Y where H = X (X^T X)^{-1} X^T. The PREDICTION at a NEW observation with predictor vector x_0^T = (1, x_{01}, ..., x_{0p}) is Yhat_0 = x_0^T beta_hat. The VARIANCE of this prediction (estimating the MEAN RESPONSE mu_0 = x_0^T beta) is Var(Yhat_0 | X) = sigma^2 x_0^T (X^T X)^{-1} x_0 — note this is a scalar (x_0^T is 1 x (p+1), (X^T X)^{-1} is (p+1) x (p+1), x_0 is (p+1) x 1). The standard error of the MEAN response is SE_mean = s sqrt(x_0^T (X^T X)^{-1} x_0), giving the 100(1-alpha)% CONFIDENCE INTERVAL for E[Y | x_0] = x_0^T beta: Yhat_0 +/- t_{n-p-1, 1-alpha/2} * s * sqrt(x_0^T (X^T X)^{-1} x_0). For a NEW INDIVIDUAL observation Y_0 = x_0^T beta + epsilon_0 (with epsilon_0 ~ N(0, sigma^2) independent of the sample), the PREDICTION ERROR is Y_0 - Yhat_0 = x_0^T (beta - beta_hat) + epsilon_0, with variance Var(Y_0 - Yhat_0) = sigma^2 x_0^T (X^T X)^{-1} x_0 + sigma^2 = sigma^2 (1 + x_0^T (X^T X)^{-1} x_0). The 100(1-alpha)% PREDICTION INTERVAL is: Yhat_0 +/- t_{n-p-1, 1-alpha/2} * s * sqrt(1 + x_0^T (X^T X)^{-1} x_0). The PI is WIDER than the CI for the mean by the extra "1 +" (irreducible noise of the new observation). As in SLR, the interval is narrowest when x_0 is at the CENTROID of the observed predictors (x_0^T (X^T X)^{-1} x_0 is minimized), and widens as x_0 moves away from the centroid in any direction — the GENERALIZATON of the bow-tie shape to (p+1)-dimensional space. EXTRAPOLATION: predicting at x_0 outside the CONVEX HULL of the observed predictor values is unreliable; the variance inflation may not be visible in x_0^T (X^T X)^{-1} x_0 alone (it captures distance from the centroid but not whether x_0 is outside the data range). Use the LEVERAGE h_00 = x_0^T (X^T X)^{-1} x_0 of the new point as a diagnostic — high h_00 (e.g., > 2(p+1)/n) signals extrapolation. SIMULTANEOUS INFERENCE: for predicting k new observations or constructing a confidence BAND over a region of x_0 values, use SCHEFFE (multiplier sqrt((p+1) F_{p+1, n-p-1, 1-alpha})) or BONFERRONI (multiplier t_{n-p-1, 1-alpha/(2k)}) corrections.',
  'ch08p9_mlr_fitted_prediction_intervals',
  'formula_recall',
  ['fitted values', 'prediction', 'mean response', 'prediction interval', 'extrapolation', 'centroid', 'Scheffe']
)

// ============================================================
// SECTION 3 — GAUSS-MARKOV THEOREM & PROPERTIES OF OLS (7 items)
// ============================================================
add(
  'What are the Gauss-Markov assumptions for linear regression?',
  'The GAUSS-MARKOV (GM) ASSUMPTIONS are the minimal conditions under which the OLS estimator is the Best Linear Unbiased Estimator (BLUE) of beta in the linear model Y = X beta + epsilon. The assumptions are: (GM1) LINEARITY IN PARAMETERS — the model is correctly specified as Y = X beta + epsilon for some beta (the conditional mean E[Y | X] is linear in beta, i.e., lies in the column space of X); (GM2) RANDOM SAMPLING / EXOGENEITY — E[epsilon | X] = 0 (the errors have mean zero conditional on the predictors; equivalently, the predictors are uncorrelated with the errors — there are no omitted confounders correlated with X); (GM3) NO PERFECT MULTICOLLINEARITY — X has full column rank p+1 (so X^T X is invertible); (GM4) SPHERICAL ERRORS / HOMOSCEDASTICITY & NO AUTOCORRELATION — Var(epsilon | X) = sigma^2 I_n (constant variance sigma^2 across observations, and zero covariance between distinct errors). NOTE: NORMALITY (epsilon | X ~ N(0, sigma^2 I)) is NOT a Gauss-Markov assumption — it is an additional assumption used for EXACT FINITE-SAMPLE INFERENCE (t-tests, F-tests, exact CIs). Without normality, GM alone gives unbiasedness and BLUE but inference relies on large-sample (asymptotic) results via the CLT. CONSEQUENCES: (1) GM1+GM2+GM3 => UNBIASEDNESS: E[beta_hat | X] = beta; (2) GM1+GM2+GM3+GM4 => GAUSS-MARKOV THEOREM: beta_hat is BLUE (minimum-variance among linear unbiased estimators, componentwise); (3) adding NORMALITY => beta_hat is the MAXIMUM LIKELIHOOD estimator and exact t/F inference is valid. WEAKENING each assumption: dropping GM4 (heteroscedastic or correlated errors) — OLS is still unbiased but no longer efficient; use GLS (Aitken) or OLS with ROBUST (sandwich) standard errors; dropping GM2 (endogeneity, omitted variables) — OLS is BIASED and INCONSISTENT; use INSTRUMENTAL VARIABLES (IV) / 2SLS; dropping GM1 (model misspecification, e.g., wrong functional form) — OLS is biased and inconsistent; add polynomial terms or use nonlinear models; dropping GM3 — beta_hat is not unique; drop collinear columns or use regularization. RANDOM vs FIXED X: GM4 is often written as Var(epsilon) = sigma^2 I (unconditional) for fixed-X designs; for random-X, the conditional form Var(epsilon | X) = sigma^2 I is the correct statement (and all GM results hold conditionally on X).',
  'ch08p9_gauss_markov_assumptions',
  'formula_recall',
  ['Gauss-Markov assumptions', 'linearity', 'exogeneity', 'homoscedasticity', 'no multicollinearity', 'BLUE']
)
add(
  'What is the Gauss-Markov theorem and what does it state about OLS?',
  'The GAUSS-MARKOV THEOREM states: Under the Gauss-Markov assumptions (linearity, exogeneity E[epsilon | X] = 0, full rank of X, and spherical errors Var(epsilon | X) = sigma^2 I_n), the OLS estimator beta_hat = (X^T X)^{-1} X^T Y is the BEST LINEAR UNBIASED ESTIMATOR (BLUE) of beta. Concretely, for any other LINEAR UNBIASED estimator beta_tilde = A Y (where A is a (p+1) x n matrix, possibly depending on X but not on Y or beta) with E[beta_tilde | X] = beta, we have Var(beta_tilde | X) - Var(beta_hat | X) = sigma^2 A (I - H) A^T, which is a POSITIVE SEMI-DEFINITE matrix (and hence, in particular, Var(beta_tilde_j | X) >= Var(beta_hat_j | X) for each component j). PROOF SKETCH: any linear unbiased estimator can be written as beta_tilde = (C + D) Y where C = (X^T X)^{-1} X^T (the OLS coefficient) and D is a matrix with D X = 0 (the unbiasedness constraint requires (C + D) X = I, so D X = I - C X = I - I = 0). Then Var(beta_tilde) = sigma^2 (C + D)(C + D)^T = sigma^2 (C C^T + D D^T + C D^T + D C^T). The cross terms vanish: C D^T = (X^T X)^{-1} X^T D^T = (X^T X)^{-1} (D X)^T = 0, similarly D C^T = 0. So Var(beta_tilde) = sigma^2 C C^T + sigma^2 D D^T = Var(beta_hat) + sigma^2 D D^T >= Var(beta_hat) (since D D^T is positive semidefinite). Equality holds iff D = 0, i.e., beta_tilde = beta_hat. KEY POINTS: (1) the theorem requires the assumptions to hold — under heteroscedasticity or autocorrelation, OLS is unbiased but NOT efficient (GLS / Aitken estimator is more efficient); (2) it is a FINITE-SAMPLE result (true for any n > p+1, not asymptotic); (3) it does NOT require NORMALITY — only the four GM assumptions; (4) it is restricted to LINEAR UNBIASED estimators — a nonlinear estimator (e.g., a James-Stein estimator) can have lower MSE (trading bias for variance reduction), but it is not "linear"; (5) it does NOT require the model to be correctly specified beyond linearity in parameters — if the conditional mean is truly X beta, OLS is BLUE; if the true mean is non-linear (e.g., E[Y|X] = g(X beta) for non-identity g), OLS is biased. IMPORTANCE: GM justifies the prominence of OLS — among linear unbiased estimators, you cannot do better under the GM assumptions; for inference, OLS with appropriate standard errors is the workhorse of empirical work.',
  'ch08p9_gauss_markov_theorem',
  'formula_recall',
  ['Gauss-Markov theorem', 'BLUE', 'best linear unbiased', 'minimum variance', 'proof', 'finite sample']
)
add(
  'What is the variance-covariance matrix of the OLS estimator and how is it estimated?',
  'Under the GM assumptions, the COVARIANCE MATRIX of beta_hat is Var(beta_hat | X) = sigma^2 (X^T X)^{-1}. DERIVATION: beta_hat = (X^T X)^{-1} X^T Y is linear in Y, so Var(beta_hat | X) = (X^T X)^{-1} X^T Var(Y | X) X (X^T X)^{-1} = (X^T X)^{-1} X^T (sigma^2 I_n) X (X^T X)^{-1} = sigma^2 (X^T X)^{-1} X^T X (X^T X)^{-1} = sigma^2 (X^T X)^{-1}. COMPONENT FORM: Var(beta_j_hat) = sigma^2 [(X^T X)^{-1}]_{jj} (the j-th diagonal entry); Cov(beta_j_hat, beta_k_hat) = sigma^2 [(X^T X)^{-1}]_{jk} (off-diagonal). In SLR, this specializes to Var(beta_1_hat) = sigma^2 / S_xx, Var(beta_0_hat) = sigma^2 (1/n + xbar^2 / S_xx), Cov(beta_0_hat, beta_1_hat) = -sigma^2 xbar / S_xx. ESTIMATION: sigma^2 is unknown, so we plug in s^2 = SSE / (n - p - 1) = e^T e / (n - p - 1), giving the ESTIMATED COVARIANCE MATRIX Var_hat(beta_hat) = s^2 (X^T X)^{-1}. The STANDARD ERRORS are SE(beta_j_hat) = s sqrt([(X^T X)^{-1}]_{jj}). DISTRIBUTION under normality: (n - p - 1) s^2 (X^T X)^{-1} / Var(beta_hat) ... no — under normality, beta_hat | X ~ N_{p+1}(beta, sigma^2 (X^T X)^{-1}), so beta_hat_j | X ~ N(beta_j, sigma^2 [(X^T X)^{-1}]_{jj}), and (beta_hat_j - beta_j) / SE(beta_hat_j) ~ t_{n-p-1} (because (n - p - 1) s^2 / sigma^2 ~ chi^2_{n-p-1} independently of beta_hat). ROBUST VARIANCE ESTIMATORS (when homoscedasticity fails): the HUBER-WHITE "SANDWICH" estimator is Var_HW(beta_hat) = (X^T X)^{-1} X^T diag(e_i^2) X (X^T X)^{-1}, which consistently estimates Var(beta_hat) under heteroscedasticity of unknown form (use HC0, HC1, HC2, HC3 variants for finite-sample correction; HC3 is preferred for small samples). CLUSTERED standard errors extend this to within-cluster correlation (e.g., panel data with observations correlated within units). HAC (Heteroscedasticity-Autocorrelation-Consistent) estimators (Newey-West) handle time-series correlation. THE TRADE-OFF: the classical s^2 (X^T X)^{-1} is more efficient if homoscedasticity holds, but is INCORRECT (gives misleading SEs) if it does not; the sandwich is more robust but slightly less efficient when homoscedasticity holds.',
  'ch08p9_ols_variance_covariance',
  'formula_recall',
  ['covariance matrix', 'X transpose X inverse', 'standard error', 'Huber-White', 'sandwich', 'HC3', 'clustered']
)
add(
  'How do you show that OLS is unbiased under the Gauss-Markov assumptions?',
  'UNBIASEDNESS of OLS: under the GM assumptions (specifically, linearity Y = X beta + epsilon and exogeneity E[epsilon | X] = 0), the OLS estimator beta_hat = (X^T X)^{-1} X^T Y is an UNBIASED estimator of beta. PROOF (conditional on X): E[beta_hat | X] = E[(X^T X)^{-1} X^T Y | X] = (X^T X)^{-1} X^T E[Y | X] (linearity of conditional expectation, treating X as fixed). Using the model Y = X beta + epsilon, E[Y | X] = E[X beta + epsilon | X] = X beta + E[epsilon | X] = X beta + 0 = X beta (the exogeneity assumption E[epsilon | X] = 0). Substituting back: E[beta_hat | X] = (X^T X)^{-1} X^T (X beta) = (X^T X)^{-1} (X^T X) beta = I_{p+1} beta = beta. UNCONDITIONAL unbiasedness follows by the law of iterated expectation: E[beta_hat] = E[E[beta_hat | X]] = E[beta] = beta. KEY INSIGHT: unbiasedness requires ONLY GM1 (linearity in parameters) and GM2 (exogeneity E[epsilon | X] = 0); it does NOT require homoscedasticity, normality, or even no-autocorrelation. IMPLICATIONS: (1) if there is an OMITTED VARIABLE correlated with X (violating exogeneity), OLS is BIASED and INCONSISTENT (the bias does not vanish as n -> infinity) — this is the Omitted Variable Bias (OVB); (2) if the model is MISSPECIFIED (e.g., quadratic term omitted when the true relationship is quadratic), OLS is biased; (3) measurement error in X (errors-in-variables) violates exogeneity and biases OLS toward zero (ATTENUATION BIAS); (4) sample SELECTION bias also violates exogeneity. CONSISTENCY (large sample): under GM1 + GM2 + (X^T X)/n -> Q (positive definite, a regularity condition) + appropriate moment conditions, beta_hat is CONSISTENT: beta_hat ->_p beta as n -> infinity. The proof uses the LLN: (1/n) X^T Y = (1/n) X^T X beta + (1/n) X^T epsilon ->_p Q beta + 0 (the latter from E[X^T epsilon] = 0 and LLN). Hence beta_hat = [(1/n) X^T X]^{-1} (1/n) X^T Y ->_p Q^{-1} Q beta = beta. Asymptotic distribution: sqrt(n)(beta_hat - beta) ->_d N(0, sigma^2 Q^{-1}).',
  'ch08p9_ols_unbiasedness_proof',
  'formula_recall',
  ['unbiasedness', 'proof', 'exogeneity', 'conditional expectation', 'consistency', 'omitted variable bias']
)
add(
  'What is the Aitken estimator (generalized least squares) and when is it used?',
  'The AITKEN ESTIMATOR, also called GENERALIZED LEAST SQUARES (GLS), is the BLUE when the errors have a NON-SPHERICAL covariance matrix: Var(epsilon | X) = sigma^2 Omega, where Omega is a KNOWN positive definite n x n matrix (not necessarily sigma^2 I). The GLS estimator is beta_hat_GLS = (X^T Omega^{-1} X)^{-1} X^T Omega^{-1} Y. DERIVATION (TRANSFORMATION to OLS): since Omega is positive definite, there exists a non-singular matrix P such that P^T P = Omega^{-1} (e.g., Cholesky: Omega^{-1} = L L^T, take P = L^T; or eigendecomposition). Transform the model: Y* = P Y, X* = P X, epsilon* = P epsilon. Then Var(epsilon*) = P Var(epsilon) P^T = P (sigma^2 Omega) P^T = sigma^2 P Omega P^T = sigma^2 P (P^T P)^{-1} P^T = sigma^2 P P^{-1} (P^T)^{-1} P^T = sigma^2 I (spherical!). Applying OLS to the transformed model: beta_hat_GLS = (X*^T X*)^{-1} X*^T Y* = ((P X)^T (P X))^{-1} (P X)^T (P Y) = (X^T P^T P X)^{-1} X^T P^T P Y = (X^T Omega^{-1} X)^{-1} X^T Omega^{-1} Y. PROPERTIES: (1) UNBIASED: E[beta_hat_GLS | X] = beta (same proof as OLS, since the transformation is linear and the transformed model satisfies GM assumptions); (2) COVARIANCE: Var(beta_hat_GLS | X) = sigma^2 (X^T Omega^{-1} X)^{-1}; (3) GAUSS-MARKOV (Aitken theorem): among LINEAR UNBIASED estimators, GLS has the SMALLEST variance when Var(epsilon) = sigma^2 Omega — this is the GENERALIZATION of the Gauss-Markov theorem (which is the special case Omega = I); (4) if Omega is correctly specified and known, GLS is more efficient than OLS. WHEN TO USE: (a) HETEROSCEDASTICITY of KNOWN form (e.g., Var(epsilon_i) = sigma^2 / w_i, where w_i is a known weight) — use WEIGHTED LEAST SQUARES (WLS), a special case of GLS with diagonal Omega; (b) AUTOCORRELATION of known structure (e.g., AR(1) errors in time series, Omega_{ij} = rho^{|i-j|}); (c) PANEL DATA with random effects (Omega captures within-unit correlation). PRACTICAL ISSUE: Omega is usually UNKNOWN. FEASIBLE GLS (FGLS) estimates Omega from the data (e.g., estimate the variance function from OLS residuals, then plug into WLS; or estimate the AR(1) coefficient rho, then transform). FGLS is asymptotically efficient but can have poor finite-sample properties if the variance model is misspecified. ALTERNATIVE: OLS with ROBUST (Huber-White) standard errors is consistent under heteroscedasticity of unknown form, with little efficiency loss compared to FGLS — often preferred in modern practice due to its robustness.',
  'ch08p9_aitken_gls_estimator',
  'formula_recall',
  ['Aitken', 'generalized least squares', 'GLS', 'Omega', 'weighted least squares', 'feasible GLS', 'transformation']
)
add(
  'What is omitted variable bias and how does it arise in regression?',
  'OMITTED VARIABLE BIAS (OVB) occurs when a relevant predictor is left out of a regression model, AND that omitted variable is CORRELATED with the included predictors. CONSEQUENCE: the OLS estimator of the included coefficients is BIASED and INCONSISTENT — the bias does not vanish as n -> infinity. FORMAL SETUP: suppose the TRUE model is Y = X beta + Z gamma + epsilon, where X is included in the regression but Z is OMITTED, and E[epsilon | X, Z] = 0. The researcher fits the SHORT model Y = X beta_tilde + u (with u = Z gamma + epsilon). The OLS estimator of beta_tilde from the short regression is beta_tilde_hat = (X^T X)^{-1} X^T Y = (X^T X)^{-1} X^T (X beta + Z gamma + epsilon) = beta + (X^T X)^{-1} X^T Z gamma + (X^T X)^{-1} X^T epsilon. Taking conditional expectation (on X, Z): E[beta_tilde_hat | X, Z] = beta + (X^T X)^{-1} X^T Z gamma + 0 = beta + (X^T X)^{-1} X^T Z gamma. The BIAS is (X^T X)^{-1} X^T Z gamma — it has two parts: (1) the projection of Z onto X (i.e., the regression of the omitted on the included), and (2) the true effect gamma of Z on Y. In the SIMPLE case of one included x and one omitted z: bias of beta_tilde_hat = gamma * Cov(x, z) / Var(x), so the sign of the bias depends on the SIGN of gamma * Cov(x, z). EXAMPLES: (a) estimating the return to education without controlling for ability — ability is omitted, education is correlated with ability (more educated people tend to be more able), and ability affects earnings (gamma > 0), so the OLS estimate of education\'s effect is BIASED UPWARD; (b) estimating the effect of police on crime without controlling for city characteristics — cities with high crime rates hire more police (positive Cov), and the true effect of police is negative (gamma < 0), so OLS UNDERESTIMATES the crime-reducing effect of police (bias is positive: Cov * gamma < 0). REMEDIES: (1) INCLUDE the omitted variable (if measurable); (2) use a PROXY variable for the omitted variable (with caveats about measurement error); (3) use INSTRUMENTAL VARIABLES (IV) that are correlated with X but uncorrelated with the omitted Z (and epsilon); (4) use PANEL DATA with FIXED EFFECTS to absorb time-invariant omitted variables; (5) use RANDOMIZED CONTROL (true experiments eliminate OVB by breaking the X-Z correlation). TEST for OVB: not directly possible (the omitted variable is unobserved); sensitivity analyses (e.g., Oster\'s delta) quantify how strong an unmeasured confounder would need to be to explain away an effect.',
  'ch08p9_omitted_variable_bias',
  'formula_recall',
  ['omitted variable bias', 'OVB', 'consistency', 'confounder', 'instrumental variable', 'fixed effects']
)
add(
  'What is the asymptotic distribution of the OLS estimator under weak assumptions?',
  'Under WEAK ASSUMPTIONS (no normality, possibly heteroscedastic errors), the OLS estimator is ASYMPTOTICALLY NORMAL with a distribution that can be used for large-sample inference. ASSUMPTIONS: (W1) LINEAR MODEL Y = X beta + epsilon with E[epsilon_i | X_i] = 0 (exogeneity); (W2) iid observations {(X_i, Y_i)} (or stationary weakly dependent for time series); (W3) E[X_i X_i^T] = Q is finite and positive definite (no perfect multicollinearity, finite second moments); (W4) E[epsilon_i^2 X_i X_i^T] = Sigma exists (a (p+1) x (p+1) matrix; allows heteroscedasticity of unknown form). CONSISTENCY: by the LLN, (1/n) X^T X ->_p Q and (1/n) X^T epsilon ->_p E[X_i epsilon_i] = 0 (using exogeneity). Therefore beta_hat = (X^T X)^{-1} X^T Y = beta + (X^T X)^{-1} X^T epsilon = beta + [(1/n) X^T X]^{-1} (1/n) X^T epsilon ->_p beta + Q^{-1} * 0 = beta (CONSISTENT). ASYMPTOTIC NORMALITY: by the multivariate CLT, (1/sqrt(n)) X^T epsilon = (1/sqrt(n)) sum X_i epsilon_i ->_d N(0, Sigma), where Sigma = E[X_i X_i^T epsilon_i^2] (the variance of X_i epsilon_i). Therefore sqrt(n) (beta_hat - beta) = sqrt(n) [(1/n) X^T X]^{-1} (1/n) X^T epsilon = [(1/n) X^T X]^{-1} (1/sqrt(n)) X^T epsilon ->_d Q^{-1} N(0, Sigma) = N(0, Q^{-1} Sigma Q^{-1}). So sqrt(n) (beta_hat - beta) ->_d N(0, V) where V = Q^{-1} Sigma Q^{-1} is the asymptotic variance (the "sandwich" form). HOMOSCEDASTIC SPECIAL CASE: if Var(epsilon_i | X_i) = sigma^2 (constant), then Sigma = sigma^2 Q, so V = Q^{-1} (sigma^2 Q) Q^{-1} = sigma^2 Q^{-1} — the classical OLS variance. ESTIMATION: plug in (1/n) X^T X for Q and (1/n) sum X_i X_i^T e_i^2 for Sigma (using OLS residuals e_i). This gives the HUBER-WHITE / SANDWICH estimator: V_hat = (X^T X)^{-1} X^T diag(e_i^2) X (X^T X)^{-1}, which is consistent under heteroscedasticity of unknown form. INFERENCE: for large n, beta_hat_j approx~ N(beta_j, V_hat_{jj}), so (beta_hat_j - beta_j) / sqrt(V_hat_{jj}) approx~ N(0, 1), and Wald tests / CIs use the standard normal quantiles. For small samples, use the t-distribution with n - p - 1 df as an approximation. PRACTICAL IMPLICATION: even when homoscedasticity fails, OLS estimates are consistent and asymptotically normal — only the STANDARD ERRORS need to be corrected (use HC0/HC1/HC2/HC3 robust SEs). This is why ROBUST STANDARD ERRORS are so widely used in empirical economics and social science.',
  'ch08p9_ols_asymptotic_distribution',
  'formula_recall',
  ['asymptotic distribution', 'CLT', 'consistency', 'Huber-White', 'sandwich', 'heteroscedasticity', 'large sample']
)

// ============================================================
// SECTION 4 — INFERENCE & HYPOTHESIS TESTING IN REGRESSION (7 items)
// ============================================================
add(
  'What is the t-test for an individual coefficient in multiple linear regression?',
  'In MLR with normality, the INDIVIDUAL t-TEST tests whether a single coefficient beta_j is equal to a specified value (usually 0). HYPOTHESES: H_0: beta_j = beta_j^{(0)} (often beta_j^{(0)} = 0) vs H_a: beta_j != beta_j^{(0)} (two-sided) or one-sided alternatives. TEST STATISTIC: T = (beta_hat_j - beta_j^{(0)}) / SE(beta_hat_j), where SE(beta_hat_j) = s sqrt([(X^T X)^{-1}]_{jj}) is the standard error (s = sqrt(SSE/(n-p-1)) is the residual standard error). DISTRIBUTION under H_0: T ~ t_{n - p - 1} (the t-distribution with n - p - 1 ERROR DEGREES OF FREEDOM), derived from (a) beta_hat_j | X ~ N(beta_j, sigma^2 [(X^T X)^{-1}]_{jj}) under normality; (b) (n - p - 1) s^2 / sigma^2 ~ chi^2_{n-p-1} independent of beta_hat_j; (c) the ratio of a standard normal to an independent sqrt(chi^2 / df) is a t-distribution. REJECTION RULE (two-sided, level alpha): reject H_0 if |T| > t_{n-p-1, 1-alpha/2}; the p-value is 2 P(T_{n-p-1} > |T_obs|). CONFIDENCE INTERVAL: 100(1-alpha)% CI for beta_j is beta_hat_j +/- t_{n-p-1, 1-alpha/2} * SE(beta_hat_j). INTERPRETATION of beta_j = 0 test: "controlling for all other predictors, x_j has no linear effect on Y." The t-test for beta_j = 0 is equivalent to the PARTIAL F-TEST for the single restriction beta_j = 0 (T^2 = F with (1, n-p-1) df). WARNING: the t-test for beta_j = 0 is NOT a test of "the marginal effect of x_j on Y ignoring other predictors" — that would require regressing Y on x_j alone (giving a different coefficient and test). The MLR t-test is the test of the CONDITIONAL (partial) effect, holding other variables constant. NUMERICAL EXAMPLE: if beta_hat_j = 2.5, SE = 1.0, n = 30, p = 3 (so df = 26), then T = 2.5; the two-sided p-value is 2 * P(T_{26} > 2.5) ~= 0.020, so reject at alpha = 0.05. The 95% CI is 2.5 +/- 2.056 * 1.0 = [0.444, 4.556]. CAVEAT: testing many coefficients at level alpha inflates the FAMILY-WISE error rate; use BONFERRONI (test each at alpha/k) or other multiple-comparison corrections. ASYMPTOTIC version: for large n, T ~ approx N(0, 1) under H_0; the t_{n-p-1} quantiles converge to the standard normal quantiles, so the distinction is moot for large samples.',
  'ch08p9_t_test_individual_coefficient',
  'formula_recall',
  ['t-test', 'individual coefficient', 'standard error', 'partial effect', 'degrees of freedom', 'p-value']
)
add(
  'What is the overall F-test for significance of the regression?',
  'The OVERALL F-TEST in MLR tests whether the regression model has ANY explanatory power — i.e., whether AT LEAST ONE of the non-intercept predictors has a non-zero coefficient. HYPOTHESES: H_0: beta_1 = beta_2 = ... = beta_p = 0 (all slopes are zero; the model reduces to the intercept-only model E[Y] = beta_0) vs H_a: at least one beta_j != 0 (j = 1, ..., p). TEST STATISTIC: F = (SSR / p) / (SSE / (n - p - 1)) = MSR / MSE, where SSR = sum (Yhat_i - Ybar)^2 (regression sum of squares, df = p), SSE = sum (Y_i - Yhat_i)^2 (error sum of squares, df = n - p - 1), MSR = SSR / p, MSE = SSE / (n - p - 1) = s^2. DISTRIBUTION under H_0: F ~ F_{p, n - p - 1} (the F-distribution with (p, n - p - 1) degrees of freedom), derived as the ratio of two independent chi-squared variables divided by their degrees of freedom (SSR/sigma^2 and SSE/sigma^2 are independent chi^2 under H_0 and normality). REJECTION RULE (level alpha): reject H_0 if F > F_{p, n - p - 1, 1 - alpha}; the p-value is P(F_{p, n-p-1} > F_obs). EQUIVALENCE TO R^2: F = (R^2 / p) / ((1 - R^2) / (n - p - 1)) = (n - p - 1) R^2 / (p (1 - R^2)), so a high R^2 corresponds to a high F. RELATION TO t-TEST IN SLR: in SLR (p = 1), F = T^2 where T is the t-statistic for beta_1 = 0 (because F_{1, n-2} = t^2_{n-2}); the overall F-test and the slope t-test give the same p-value. INTERPRETATION: rejecting H_0 means the model explains a significant fraction of the variance in Y, but does NOT mean the model is "correct" or that all predictors are individually significant (some beta_j might be 0). FAILING to reject H_0 means the model has no detectable explanatory power — predictors are not jointly useful for predicting Y. CAVEATS: (1) the test assumes the GM assumptions (especially normality for exact F distribution; otherwise large-sample Wald test); (2) a significant F does not validate the model — check diagnostics (residual plots, leverage, etc.); (3) with many predictors and small n, the F-test may have low power. ALTERNATIVE FORM (likelihood ratio): under normality, the F-test is equivalent to the LIKELIHOOD RATIO test for H_0: beta_1 = ... = beta_p = 0; F = ((SSE_reduced - SSE_full) / p) / (SSE_full / (n - p - 1)) where SSE_reduced is from the intercept-only model (= SST) and SSE_full is from the MLR model.',
  'ch08p9_overall_f_test',
  'formula_recall',
  ['overall F-test', 'significance of regression', 'MSR', 'MSE', 'R-squared', 'likelihood ratio']
)
add(
  'What is the partial F-test for comparing nested regression models?',
  'The PARTIAL F-TEST (also called the EXTRA SUM OF SQUARES F-test) compares two NESTED regression models: a FULL model with p+1 parameters (including intercept) and a REDUCED model with q+1 parameters (q < p), where the reduced model is a SPECIAL CASE of the full model (obtained by setting p - q of the full model\'s coefficients to zero). HYPOTHESES: H_0: the p - q "extra" coefficients are all zero (the reduced model is adequate) vs H_a: at least one of the extra coefficients is non-zero (the full model is needed). TEST STATISTIC: F = ((SSE_reduced - SSE_full) / (p - q)) / (SSE_full / (n - p - 1)), where SSE_reduced is the SSE of the reduced model (df = n - q - 1), SSE_full is the SSE of the full model (df = n - p - 1), and p - q = (df_reduced - df_full) is the number of restrictions. DISTRIBUTION under H_0: F ~ F_{p - q, n - p - 1}. REJECTION RULE: reject H_0 if F > F_{p - q, n - p - 1, 1 - alpha}; p-value = P(F_{p-q, n-p-1} > F_obs). INTERPRETATION: rejecting H_0 means the FULL model fits SIGNIFICANTLY better than the reduced model — the extra predictors add explanatory power beyond the reduced model. EXAMPLES: (1) testing the overall significance of a categorical predictor with k levels: full model has k-1 dummies, reduced model has 0 dummies (p - q = k - 1 restrictions); (2) testing an INTERACTION: full model has main effects + interactions, reduced has only main effects; (3) testing polynomial terms: full model has x, x^2, x^3; reduced has only x (p - q = 2). EQUIVALENCE TO WALD TEST: under normality, the partial F-test is equivalent to a WALD TEST on the vector of extra coefficients; F = (1/(p-q)) * (extra_beta_hat)^T [Var_hat(extra_beta_hat)]^{-1} (extra_beta_hat). RELATION TO R^2: F = ((R^2_full - R^2_reduced) / (p - q)) / ((1 - R^2_full) / (n - p - 1)) — the increase in R^2 from adding the extra predictors, scaled by the remaining unexplained variance. NOTE: the partial F-test requires NESTED models (the reduced model is a special case of the full); for NON-NESTED models, use other criteria (AIC, BIC, Vuong test, Cox test, J test). STEPWISE MODEL SELECTION uses partial F-tests iteratively: at each step, test the marginal contribution of adding (forward) or dropping (backward) one predictor; stop when no significant changes (with a chosen threshold, often called the "F-to-enter" and "F-to-remove").',
  'ch08p9_partial_f_test_nested',
  'formula_recall',
  ['partial F-test', 'nested models', 'extra sum of squares', 'Wald test', 'R-squared change', 'stepwise']
)
add(
  'What is the Wald test for linear restrictions on regression coefficients?',
  'The WALD TEST tests general LINEAR RESTRICTIONS on the coefficient vector beta in MLR. SETUP: H_0: R beta = r vs H_a: R beta != r, where R is a q x (p+1) matrix of known constants (q = number of restrictions, rank(R) = q), and r is a q x 1 vector of known constants. EXAMPLES: (a) H_0: beta_j = c (single coefficient): R = [0, ..., 1, ..., 0] (1 in position j), r = [c], q = 1; (b) H_0: beta_1 = beta_2 (equality of two coefficients): R = [0, 1, -1, 0, ..., 0], r = [0]; (c) H_0: beta_1 + beta_2 = 1 (e.g., constant returns to scale in a Cobb-Douglas): R = [0, 1, 1, 0, ..., 0], r = [1]; (d) H_0: beta_1 = beta_2 = ... = beta_p = 0 (overall F-test): R = [0_{p x 1} | I_p], r = 0_{p x 1}, q = p. TEST STATISTIC: W = (R beta_hat - r)^T [R Var_hat(beta_hat) R^T]^{-1} (R beta_hat - r), where Var_hat(beta_hat) = s^2 (X^T X)^{-1} (under homoscedasticity) or the robust sandwich estimator. DISTRIBUTION under H_0: (a) EXACT (under normality, q = 1): W / 1 = T^2 ~ F_{1, n-p-1} = t^2_{n-p-1} (equivalent to t-test); (b) EXACT (under normality, general q): (W / q) / (s^2 / sigma^2) ... the exact finite-sample form is F = (W / q) ~ F_{q, n - p - 1} (under normality); (c) ASYMPTOTIC (without normality, large n): W ->_d chi^2_q (the chi-squared approximation). REJECTION RULE: reject H_0 if W > chi^2_{q, 1 - alpha} (large sample) or if F = W/q > F_{q, n-p-1, 1-alpha} (finite sample with normality). INTUITION: W measures the SQUARED DISTANCE between R beta_hat and r, weighted by the inverse of the estimated covariance of R beta_hat — large W means the data are inconsistent with H_0. RELATION TO LIKELIHOOD RATIO (LR) TEST: LR = -2 log (L_reduced / L_full) = -2 (ell_reduced - ell_full); under H_0, LR ->_d chi^2_q asymptotically. WALD vs LR vs LAGRANGE MULTIPLIER (LM) tests are asymptotically equivalent (all -> chi^2_q) but differ in finite samples: WALD requires only the full model fit; LR requires both; LM (score) requires only the reduced model fit. PRACTICAL: most regression software reports the Wald test by default (e.g., the t-test for individual coefficients is a Wald test with q=1). WARNING: the Wald test can have poor finite-sample properties when the parameter is near the boundary of the parameter space (e.g., testing variance components = 0) — use the LR test in such cases.',
  'ch08p9_wald_test_linear_restrictions',
  'formula_recall',
  ['Wald test', 'linear restrictions', 'R beta = r', 'chi-squared', 'F-test', 'LR test', 'LM test']
)
add(
  'How do you construct confidence intervals and confidence bands for regression?',
  'In MLR, there are several types of CONFIDENCE INTERVALS and BANDS, each with a different estimand: (1) CI for INDIVIDUAL COEFFICIENT beta_j: 100(1-alpha)% CI is beta_hat_j +/- t_{n-p-1, 1-alpha/2} * SE(beta_hat_j), where SE(beta_hat_j) = s sqrt([(X^T X)^{-1}]_{jj}). INTERPRETATION: covers beta_j in 100(1-alpha)% of repeated samples. (2) CI for a LINEAR COMBINATION c^T beta (e.g., a contrast, or the mean response at a single x_0 = c): c^T beta_hat +/- t_{n-p-1, 1-alpha/2} * s sqrt(c^T (X^T X)^{-1} c). (3) CI for the MEAN RESPONSE E[Y | x_0] = x_0^T beta at a single point x_0: Yhat_0 +/- t_{n-p-1, 1-alpha/2} * s sqrt(x_0^T (X^T X)^{-1} x_0). (4) SIMULTANEOUS CI for the mean response at MULTIPLE points x_1, ..., x_k (BONFERRONI): use multiplier t_{n-p-1, 1-alpha/(2k)} instead of t_{n-p-1, 1-alpha/2} for each individual CI; covers all k means simultaneously with coverage >= 1 - alpha. (5) SCHEFFE CONFIDENCE BAND for the entire regression surface E[Y | x] = x^T beta over a region R of x values: Yhat(x) +/- sqrt((p+1) F_{p+1, n-p-1, 1-alpha}) * s sqrt(x^T (X^T X)^{-1} x), for ALL x in R simultaneously. The Scheffe band has simultaneous coverage 1 - alpha over the ENTIRE region R (a hyper-ellipsoid in the predictor space), making it the most conservative but the most broadly applicable. (6) WORKING-HOTELLING BAND (specialized for the MEAN response over all x in R^p): Yhat(x) +/- W * s sqrt(x^T (X^T X)^{-1} x), where W = sqrt((p+1) F_{p+1, n-p-1, 1-alpha}) (same multiplier as Scheffe in this case). (7) JOINT CONFIDENCE REGION for the entire coefficient vector beta: the 100(1-alpha)% region is the ELLIPSOID {beta : (beta_hat - beta)^T X^T X (beta_hat - beta) / ((p+1) s^2) <= F_{p+1, n-p-1, 1-alpha}}. PROJECTION of this ellipsoid onto individual axes gives SIMULTANEOUS CIs for each beta_j (Scheffe-adjusted): beta_hat_j +/- sqrt((p+1) F_{p+1, n-p-1, 1-alpha}) * SE(beta_hat_j). CHOOSING: use (1) for single coefficient inference; use (3) for a single new observation\'s mean; use (4) (Bonferroni) for a small number of pre-specified points; use (5) (Scheffe) when you want coverage over an entire region or when you peeked at the data before choosing the points (data-snooping resistant).',
  'ch08p9_confidence_intervals_bands',
  'formula_recall',
  ['confidence interval', 'confidence band', 'Scheffe', 'Working-Hotelling', 'Bonferroni', 'joint region', 'ellipsoid']
)
add(
  'What are prediction intervals for new observations in regression and how do they differ from confidence intervals?',
  'In MLR, a PREDICTION INTERVAL (PI) for a NEW observation Y_0 = x_0^T beta + epsilon_0 (with epsilon_0 ~ N(0, sigma^2) INDEPENDENT of the sample) at predictor value x_0 is: Yhat_0 +/- t_{n-p-1, 1-alpha/2} * s * sqrt(1 + x_0^T (X^T X)^{-1} x_0), where Yhat_0 = x_0^T beta_hat is the point prediction. CONTRAST WITH CI for the MEAN RESPONSE E[Y | x_0] = x_0^T beta: Yhat_0 +/- t_{n-p-1, 1-alpha/2} * s * sqrt(x_0^T (X^T X)^{-1} x_0) (note the ABSENCE of the "1 +"). The difference is the extra "1" inside the square root, representing the variance sigma^2 of the new error term epsilon_0. CONSEQUENTLY: (1) the PI is always WIDER than the CI for the mean (at the same x_0); (2) as n -> infinity, the CI for the mean shrinks to a point (sigma^2 (X^T X)^{-1} -> 0 because (X^T X) -> infinity), but the PI approaches Yhat_0 +/- z_{1-alpha/2} sigma — there is an IRREDUCIBLE component sigma from epsilon_0 that does not vanish. EXPLANATION: the CI quantifies uncertainty about E[Y | x_0] = x_0^T beta (a fixed but unknown parameter); the PI quantifies uncertainty about the actual VALUE of a new observation Y_0 = mu_0 + epsilon_0, which has both parameter uncertainty and the irreducible noise epsilon_0. The variance of the PREDICTION ERROR Y_0 - Yhat_0 = (mu_0 - Yhat_0) + epsilon_0 has two components: Var(Yhat_0) (parameter uncertainty) and Var(epsilon_0) = sigma^2 (irreducible noise); they add (independence): Var(Y_0 - Yhat_0) = sigma^2 x_0^T (X^T X)^{-1} x_0 + sigma^2 = sigma^2 (1 + x_0^T (X^T X)^{-1} x_0). BOW-TIE / HOURGLASS SHAPE: both the CI and PI are narrowest at x_0 = xbar (the centroid of the observed predictors) and WIDEN as x_0 moves away — reflecting greater uncertainty in extrapolation. TOLERANCE INTERVALS: a different concept — an interval that captures at least a proportion P of the population with confidence 1-alpha (combines both parameter uncertainty and the spread of the population). For normal errors, a two-sided tolerance interval is Yhat_0 +/- K * s where K depends on P, 1-alpha, n, p (computed via noncentral t or normal approximations). Tolerance intervals are used in manufacturing / quality control to specify acceptable ranges for individual items.',
  'ch08p9_prediction_vs_confidence_intervals',
  'formula_recall',
  ['prediction interval', 'confidence interval', 'mean response', 'irreducible noise', 'tolerance interval', 'bow-tie']
)
add(
  'What is the likelihood ratio test in regression and how does it relate to the F-test?',
  'The LIKELIHOOD RATIO (LR) TEST in regression compares two NESTED models via their maximized log-likelihoods. SETUP: full model M_full with p+1 parameters (log-likelihood ell_full); reduced model M_reduced with q+1 < p+1 parameters (log-likelihood ell_reduced, where the reduced model is a special case of the full). HYPOTHESES: H_0: the p - q "extra" coefficients are zero. TEST STATISTIC: LR = -2 (ell_reduced - ell_full) = -2 log (L_reduced / L_full) = 2 (ell_full - ell_reduced) (always >= 0 because the full model fits at least as well as the reduced). DISTRIBUTION under H_0: LR ->_d chi^2_{p - q} (asymptotically, by Wilks\' theorem). REJECTION RULE (large sample): reject H_0 if LR > chi^2_{p - q, 1 - alpha}; p-value = P(chi^2_{p-q} > LR_obs). EXACT FINITE-SAMPLE FORM UNDER NORMALITY: for MLR with normal errors, the LR test for linear restrictions is MONOTONICALLY RELATED to the partial F-test: LR = n log(1 + (p - q) F / (n - p - 1)), where F = ((SSE_reduced - SSE_full)/(p-q)) / (SSE_full/(n-p-1)) is the partial F-statistic. Equivalently, F = ((n - p - 1)/q) * (exp(LR/n) - 1). DERIVATION: under normality, the log-likelihood is ell(beta, sigma^2) = -(n/2) log(2 pi sigma^2) - SSE(beta)/(2 sigma^2); maximizing over sigma^2 gives sigma^2_hat = SSE/n (MLE), and ell_max = -(n/2) log(2 pi sigma^2_hat) - n/2 = -(n/2) log(SSE/n) + const. So LR = 2 (ell_full - ell_reduced) = 2 * (n/2) [log(SSE_reduced) - log(SSE_full)] = n log(SSE_reduced / SSE_full) = n log(1 + (SSE_reduced - SSE_full)/SSE_full) = n log(1 + (p - q) F / (n - p - 1)). COMPARISON OF WALD, LR, LM (the "TRINITY"): (1) WALD: W = (R beta_hat)^T [R Var_hat(beta_hat) R^T]^{-1} (R beta_hat) — requires only the FULL model; (2) LR: requires BOTH full and reduced models; (3) LM (LAGRANGE MULTIPLIER / SCORE): requires only the REDUCED model, uses the score (gradient) at the reduced-model estimate. All three are ASYMPTOTICALLY EQUIVALENT (-> chi^2_{p-q}) but differ in finite samples. INEQUALITY: W >= LR >= LM (typically), so the Wald test rejects most often and LM least. PRACTICAL: the F-test (a finite-sample exact version of LR under normality) is preferred for MLR with normal errors; the LR test is preferred for GLMs (logistic, Poisson) and other non-Gaussian models; the Wald test is computationally cheapest (only one model fit).',
  'ch08p9_likelihood_ratio_test',
  'formula_recall',
  ['likelihood ratio', 'LR test', 'Wilks theorem', 'partial F-test', 'Wald', 'LM', 'score test', 'trinity']
)

// ============================================================
// SECTION 5 — REGRESSION DIAGNOSTICS & REMEDIES (7 items)
// ============================================================
add(
  'What are residual plots and what patterns reveal model violations?',
  'RESIDUAL PLOTS are graphical tools for diagnosing model assumptions and identifying outliers in regression. The basic residual plot is e_i (residual) vs Yhat_i (fitted value) — under the correct model with constant variance, the residuals should look like a RANDOM SCATTER of points around 0, with no pattern. Common DEVIATIONS and their interpretations: (1) NON-LINEARITY (curvature): a U-shaped or curved pattern in residuals vs fitted (or vs a predictor) indicates that the linear model is missing a non-linear term — remedy: add polynomial terms (x^2, x^3), use a transformation (log Y, sqrt Y, 1/Y), or use splines / generalized additive models; (2) HETEROSCEDASTICITY (non-constant variance): a "fan" or "funnel" shape (residual spread increases or decreases with fitted values) indicates non-constant variance — remedy: transform Y (e.g., log Y stabilizes variance when Var(Y) ~ (E[Y])^2), use WEIGHTED LEAST SQUARES, or use ROBUST STANDARD ERRORS; (3) OUTLIERS: a few residuals far from 0 (e.g., |r_i| > 3 studentized) indicate unusual observations — investigate whether they are data errors or genuinely extreme; consider robust regression; (4) AUTOCORRELATION (in time-series data): residuals plotted vs time showing a pattern (runs of positive then negative residuals) suggest correlated errors — remedy: use Newey-West HAC standard errors or model the autocorrelation (AR(1) errors, GLS). OTHER DIAGNOSTIC PLOTS: (a) NORMAL Q-Q PLOT of residuals: points should fall on a straight line if normal; systematic curvature (e.g., S-shape) indicates non-normality (heavy tails or skewness); (b) RESIDUALS vs LEVERAGE (with Cook\'s distance contours): identifies influential observations; (c) SCALE-LOCATION PLOT (sqrt(|r_i|) vs Yhat_i): another check for homoscedasticity. PARTIAL RESIDUAL PLOT (component-plus-residual plot): e_i + beta_hat_j x_{ij} vs x_{ij} — shows the relationship between Y and x_j ADJUSTING for other predictors; useful for detecting non-linearity in a specific predictor. ADDED VARIABLE PLOT (partial regression plot): residuals from regressing Y on all predictors except x_j, vs residuals from regressing x_j on the other predictors — the slope is beta_hat_j, and curvature indicates non-linearity in x_j. PRACTICAL: always plot residuals — formal tests (Breusch-Pagan, White, Shapiro-Wilk) supplement but do not replace visual inspection. ROBUSTNESS: OLS inference is fairly robust to mild non-normality (by CLT for large n) but sensitive to heteroscedasticity (use robust SEs) and outliers (use robust regression).',
  'ch08p9_residual_plots_diagnostics',
  'formula_recall',
  ['residual plot', 'non-linearity', 'heteroscedasticity', 'outlier', 'Q-Q plot', 'partial residual', 'added variable']
)
add(
  'What is leverage and how is it used to identify high-leverage points?',
  'LEVERAGE in MLR measures how FAR an observation\'s predictor vector x_i is from the CENTROID of all predictor vectors (xbar = (1/n) sum x_i). Formally, the LEVERAGE of observation i is h_ii = [(H)_{ii}] = [X (X^T X)^{-1} X^T]_{ii}, the i-th DIAGONAL element of the HAT MATRIX H. PROPERTIES: (1) 1/n <= h_ii <= 1 (in MLR; in SLR h_ii = 1/n + (x_i - xbar)^2 / S_xx, where the (x_i - xbar)^2 / S_xx term measures the squared distance from the mean, scaled by the predictor spread); (2) sum_{i=1}^{n} h_{ii} = trace(H) = p + 1 (the number of parameters), so the AVERAGE leverage is (p + 1) / n; (3) h_ii measures the WEIGHT observation i places on its own fitted value: Yhat_i = sum_j h_{ij} Y_j, so h_{ii} = partial Yhat_i / partial Y_i — a high-leverage point "pulls" its fitted value toward its own Y_i. INTERPRETATION: a HIGH-LEVERAGE point is an observation whose predictor values x_i are unusual (extreme in one or more dimensions) relative to the rest of the data. It does NOT necessarily have a large residual — high leverage just means the observation has the POTENTIAL to strongly influence the regression line. RULE OF THUMB: flag observation i as high-leverage if h_ii > 2(p+1)/n (some use 3(p+1)/n for "very high"). EXAMPLE: in SLR with x_i = 100 when the rest of the data are in [0, 10], observation i has very high leverage because its x value is far from xbar. IMPACT: a high-leverage point with a SMALL residual (its Y is on the regression line defined by the rest of the data) does not change the fit much; but a high-leverage point with a LARGE residual can DRAMATICALLY tilt the regression line — this is an INFLUENTIAL point (measured by Cook\'s distance). RELATION TO RESIDUAL VARIANCE: Var(e_i | X) = sigma^2 (1 - h_{ii}), so high-leverage points have LOWER residual variance (they "fit themselves" better — the fitted value is pulled toward the observed Y). This is why STUDENTIZED RESIDUALS r_i = e_i / (s sqrt(1 - h_{ii})) inflate the residual by 1/sqrt(1 - h_{ii}) to standardize for leverage. EXTERNALLY STUDENTIZED RESIDUALS (r-i or RSTUDENT) use s_{(i)} (sigma estimate without observation i) instead of s, making them t-distributed under normality (useful for outlier testing). LEVERAGE is computed from X alone (no Y), so it can be assessed BEFORE looking at the response — useful in experimental design (avoid high-leverage design points if possible).',
  'ch08p9_leverage_high_leverage_points',
  'formula_recall',
  ['leverage', 'hat matrix diagonal', 'centroid', 'high-leverage', 'studentized residual', 'externally studentized']
)
add(
  'What is Cook\'s distance and how is it used to detect influential observations?',
  'COOK\'S DISTANCE (D_i) measures the INFLUENCE of observation i on the OLS estimator beta_hat — specifically, how much beta_hat CHANGES when observation i is DELETED from the data. DEFINITION: D_i = (beta_hat - beta_hat_{(i)})^T (X^T X) (beta_hat - beta_hat_{(i)}) / ((p+1) s^2) = sum_j (Yhat_j - Yhat_{j,(i)})^2 / ((p+1) s^2), where beta_hat_{(i)} is the OLS estimate computed WITHOUT observation i, and Yhat_{j,(i)} is the corresponding fitted value at observation j. COMPUTATIONALLY EFFICIENT FORMULA (avoiding n refits): D_i = (r_i^2 / (p+1)) * (h_{ii} / (1 - h_{ii})) = (e_i^2 / ((p+1) s^2)) * (h_{ii} / (1 - h_{ii})^2), where r_i = e_i / (s sqrt(1 - h_{ii})) is the (internally) studentized residual and h_{ii} is the leverage. DERIVATION uses the SHERMAN-MORRISON-WOODBURY formula for the rank-1 update of (X^T X)^{-1} when observation i is removed: beta_hat - beta_hat_{(i)} = (X^T X)^{-1} x_i e_i / (1 - h_{ii}). INTERPRETATION: D_i is the SQUARED STANDARDIZED DISTANCE between beta_hat and beta_hat_{(i)} in the metric of (X^T X)/s^2 — equivalently, the sum of squared changes in all fitted values, scaled by (p+1) s^2. RULES OF THUMB for flagging: (1) D_i > 1 (substantial influence — the observation moves beta_hat by more than one standard-error-ellipse); (2) D_i > 4/n (a common cutoff based on the average); (3) D_i > F_{p+1, n-p-1, 0.5} (the 50th percentile of the F distribution — the observation is "more extreme than the median F-value"); (4) visually, plot D_i vs observation index and look for points standing out. COMPARISON TO LEVERAGE: leverage (h_{ii}) is determined by X alone; influence (D_i) requires BOTH high leverage AND a large residual — D_i combines them multiplicatively (D_i ~ r_i^2 * h_{ii} / (1 - h_{ii})). A point with high leverage but small residual is NOT influential (D_i small); a point with low leverage but large residual is also NOT influential (D_i small because h_{ii} small); only HIGH LEVERAGE + LARGE RESIDUAL gives a large D_i. REMEDIES for influential observations: (1) verify data accuracy (correct typos, re-measure); (2) report results WITH and WITHOUT the influential points (sensitivity analysis); (3) use ROBUST REGRESSION (M-estimation, e.g., Huber, Tukey bisquare) which down-weights outliers; (4) consider whether the influential observation represents a different population (subgroup analysis). CAUTION: deleting influential observations should NOT be done automatically — they may contain genuinely important information about the underlying process.',
  'ch08p9_cooks_distance_influence',
  'formula_recall',
  ['Cook distance', 'influence', 'leverage', 'studentized residual', 'Sherman-Morrison', 'robust regression']
)
add(
  'What are DFFITS, DFBETAS, and COVRATIO as influence measures?',
  'In addition to Cook\'s distance, three other INFLUENCE MEASURES isolate specific effects of deleting observation i: DFFITS, DFBETAS, and COVRATIO. DFFITS (Difference in FIT, Standardized): DFFITS_i = (Yhat_i - Yhat_{i,(i)}) / (s_{(i)} sqrt(h_{ii})) = r-i * sqrt(h_{ii} / (1 - h_{ii})), where Yhat_{i,(i)} is the fitted value at observation i computed WITHOUT observation i in the fit, s_{(i)} is the residual standard error without observation i, and r-i is the EXTERNALLY studentized residual. INTERPRETATION: DFFITS_i measures how many standard deviations the fitted value Yhat_i changes when observation i is deleted. CUTOFF: |DFFITS_i| > 2 sqrt((p+1)/n) (or > 1 for small samples) flags influential points. DFBETAS (Difference in BETA, Standardized): for each coefficient j, DFBETAS_{j,i} = (beta_hat_j - beta_hat_{j,(i)}) / (s_{(i)} sqrt([(X^T X)^{-1}]_{jj})), measuring the change in beta_hat_j (in units of its standard error without observation i) when observation i is deleted. CUTOFF: |DFBETAS_{j,i}| > 2/sqrt(n) (or > 1 for small samples). DFBETAS is computed for EACH coefficient separately, giving a vector of length p+1 for each observation. COVRATIO: COVRATIO_i = det(Var_hat(beta_hat)_{(i)}) / det(Var_hat(beta_hat)) = det(s_{(i)}^2 (X_{(i)}^T X_{(i)})^{-1}) / det(s^2 (X^T X)^{-1}), the ratio of determinants of the estimated covariance matrix of beta_hat WITHOUT and WITH observation i. INTERPRETATION: COVRATIO_i > 1 means observation i IMPROVES precision (deleting it inflates the determinant); COVRATIO_i < 1 means observation i DEGRADES precision. CUTOFF: |COVRATIO_i - 1| > 3(p+1)/n flags influential points. RELATIONSHIP: all four measures (Cook, DFFITS, DFBETAS, COVRATIO) are based on the LEAVE-ONE-OUT principle and the Sherman-Morrison-Woodbury rank-1 update; they differ in WHAT they measure (overall fit, individual fits, individual coefficients, covariance matrix). COMPUTATIONAL TRICK: all leave-one-out quantities are computable from a SINGLE full-data fit using the formulas above (no need to refit n times), making these diagnostics efficient even for large datasets. PRACTICAL USE: examine Cook\'s distance for overall influence; DFFITS for influence on individual fitted values; DFBETAS for influence on specific coefficients (especially coefficients of interest in the research question); COVRATIO for effect on precision. Report sensitivity analyses: "results are similar when influential observations (Cook\'s D > 4/n) are excluded" or "results are sensitive to observation X; we report both with and without."',
  'ch08p9_dffits_dfbetas_covratio',
  'formula_recall',
  ['DFFITS', 'DFBETAS', 'COVRATIO', 'leave-one-out', 'Sherman-Morrison', 'influence measure']
)
add(
  'What are the Breusch-Pagan and White tests for heteroscedasticity?',
  'HETEROSCEDASTICITY (non-constant error variance) violates GM4 and invalidates the classical OLS standard errors (though OLS estimates remain unbiased). Two formal tests detect it. BREUSCH-PAGAN (BP) TEST: tests whether the error variance is constant (H_0: homoscedastic) against a specific alternative where Var(epsilon_i) = h(z_i^T alpha) for some function h and a vector of auxiliary variables z_i (often z_i = (1, x_{i1}, ..., x_{ip}), the predictors themselves). PROCEDURE: (1) fit OLS, obtain residuals e_i; (2) compute the OLS regression of e_i^2 on z_i (the auxiliary regression), obtaining R^2_{aux}; (3) the BP statistic is LM = n * R^2_{aux}, distributed under H_0 as chi^2_q (q = number of auxiliary regressors excluding the intercept). REJECT H_0 if LM > chi^2_{q, 1-alpha}. The KOENKER (studentized) version replaces n * R^2 with (n - q) * R^2 / 2 / (1 - mean(e^2)/...) for better small-sample properties — this is the default in most software. WHITE TEST: a more general test that does not require specifying the form of heteroscedasticity. The aux regression includes all predictors, their SQUARES, and all pairwise CROSS-PRODUCTS (interactions): e_i^2 on (1, x_1, ..., x_p, x_1^2, ..., x_p^2, x_1 x_2, ..., x_{p-1} x_p). The White statistic is again LM = n * R^2_{aux} ~ chi^2_q under H_0, where q is the number of aux regressors (excluding intercept). The White test is MORE GENERAL than BP (catches more forms of heteroscedasticity) but has LOWER POWER against specific alternatives (because it spreads its power across many possible forms) and uses many degrees of freedom when p is large. SPECIAL CASE: for SLR, White\'s test regresses e_i^2 on (1, x, x^2). INTERPRETATION: rejecting H_0 means there is evidence of heteroscedasticity; the classical OLS standard errors are INVALID (typically too small, leading to over-confidence). REMEDIES: (1) ROBUST STANDARD ERRORS (Huber-White / sandwich / HC0-HC3) — the modern default; corrects the SEs without changing the OLS point estimates; (2) WEIGHTED LEAST SQUARES (WLS) if the variance function Var(epsilon_i) = sigma^2 / w_i is known or can be estimated (Feasible GLS); (3) TRANSFORM the response (e.g., log Y stabilizes variance when Var(Y) ~ (E[Y])^2 — common in positive-skewed data like income); (4) use HETEROSCEDASTICITY-CONSISTENT inference (cluster-robust SEs for grouped data, HAC for time series). WHEN TO USE WHICH: BP is appropriate when you have a specific theory about the variance function (e.g., variance increases with income); White is appropriate as a general diagnostic. Modern practice often SKIPS the test and directly uses robust SEs (which are valid under both H_0 and H_a).',
  'ch08p9_breusch_pagan_white_tests',
  'formula_recall',
  ['Breusch-Pagan', 'White test', 'heteroscedasticity', 'Koenker', 'auxiliary regression', 'robust standard errors']
)
add(
  'What is weighted least squares (WLS) and when is it preferred over OLS?',
  'WEIGHTED LEAST SQUARES (WLS) is a special case of GENERALIZED LEAST SQUARES (GLS) used when the errors are HETEROSCEDASTIC with KNOWN (or estimated) variance structure: Var(epsilon_i | X) = sigma^2 / w_i, where w_i > 0 are KNOWN WEIGHTS. The WLS estimator minimizes the WEIGHTED RESIDUAL SUM OF SQUARES Q(beta) = sum_{i=1}^{n} w_i (Y_i - x_i^T beta)^2 (each residual is weighted by w_i — observations with HIGH variance get LOW weight, and vice versa). The WLS estimator is: beta_hat_WLS = (X^T W X)^{-1} X^T W Y, where W = diag(w_1, ..., w_n) is the diagonal weight matrix. DERIVATION: this is GLS with Omega^{-1} = W / sigma^2 (i.e., Omega = sigma^2 W^{-1}); applying the GLS formula beta_hat_GLS = (X^T Omega^{-1} X)^{-1} X^T Omega^{-1} Y gives the WLS formula. COVARIANCE: Var(beta_hat_WLS | X) = sigma^2 (X^T W X)^{-1}. GAUSS-MARKOV (Aitken theorem): if Var(epsilon_i) = sigma^2 / w_i is correctly specified, WLS is BLUE (more efficient than OLS). INTUITION: WLS gives more weight to "reliable" (low-variance) observations and less to "noisy" (high-variance) ones; in the extreme, an observation with w_i = 0 is excluded. EXAMPLES of known variance structure: (1) AGGREGATED DATA — if Y_i is the mean of m_i observations, then Var(Y_i) = sigma^2 / m_i, so use w_i = m_i (weight by group size); (2) INVERSE-VARIANCE WEIGHTING — if Var(epsilon_i) = sigma_i^2 is known (e.g., from instrument calibration), use w_i = 1/sigma_i^2 (the MINIMUM-VARIANCE choice); (3) PROPORTIONAL VARIANCE — if Var(epsilon_i) = sigma^2 x_i (variance proportional to a predictor), use w_i = 1/x_i. FEASIBLE WLS (FWLS) when variance is UNKNOWN: (1) fit OLS, obtain residuals e_i; (2) estimate the variance function Var(epsilon_i) ~ g(x_i, hat) by regressing log(e_i^2) on x_i or by fitting a model to e_i^2; (3) use w_i = 1/g(x_i, hat) in WLS. FWLS is asymptotically efficient if the variance model is correct, but can perform poorly in small samples. DIAGNOSTIC: residual plot of e_i vs Yhat_i (or vs predictors) showing a fan shape indicates heteroscedasticity — try WLS or use robust SEs. CHOICE BETWEEN WLS AND ROBUST SEs: WLS is more efficient when the variance model is KNOWN and correctly specified; robust SEs are SAFER (consistent under any form of heteroscedasticity) but slightly less efficient. Modern practice favors robust SEs as the default, with WLS used when there is a strong theoretical reason for a specific variance structure.',
  'ch08p9_weighted_least_squares',
  'formula_recall',
  ['weighted least squares', 'WLS', 'GLS', 'weights', 'feasible WLS', 'inverse-variance', 'heteroscedasticity']
)
add(
  'What are robust (Huber-White) standard errors and the HC0-HC3 variants?',
  'ROBUST STANDARD ERRORS (also called HUBER-WHITE, SANDWICH, or HETEROSCEDASTICITY-CONSISTENT standard errors) provide valid inference for OLS estimates even when the homoscedasticity assumption (GM4) is violated. The CLASSICAL OLS covariance estimator s^2 (X^T X)^{-1} is BIASED under heteroscedasticity (it assumes Var(epsilon) = sigma^2 I). The SANDWICH ESTIMATOR replaces the inner term with an empirical estimate of E[X_i X_i^T epsilon_i^2]: Var_HW(beta_hat) = (X^T X)^{-1} X^T diag(e_i^2) X (X^T X)^{-1} = "bread" * "meat" * "bread", where the "bread" is (X^T X)^{-1} and the "meat" is X^T diag(e_i^2) X = sum x_i x_i^T e_i^2. CONSISTENCY: under heteroscedasticity of unknown form, Var_HW(beta_hat) ->_p Var(beta_hat) = Q^{-1} Sigma Q^{-1} (the asymptotic variance). FINITE-SAMPLE VARIANTS (HC0-HC3): the basic form (HC0) is biased downward in finite samples (the OLS residuals e_i = Y_i - x_i^T beta_hat are SYSTEMATICALLY smaller than the true errors epsilon_i, because OLS "fits" the data). Corrections: (HC1) multiply by n / (n - p - 1) — analogous to using s^2 = SSE/(n-p-1) instead of SSE/n; (HC2) replace e_i^2 with e_i^2 / (1 - h_{ii}) — uses the leverage to "unshrink" the residual; (HC3) replace e_i^2 with e_i^2 / (1 - h_{ii})^2 — a stronger correction (LEAVE-ONE-OUT residual variance); (HC4) and (HC5) further inflate high-leverage residuals. RECOMMENDATION: HC3 is preferred for small samples (MacKinnon-White 1985) — it has the best coverage; HC1 is the Stata default. ASYMPTOTIC INFERENCE: with robust SEs, the t-statistic T = (beta_hat_j - beta_j) / SE_HW(beta_hat_j) is approximately N(0,1) for large n (or t_{n-p-1} as a small-sample approximation). CLUSTER-ROBUST SEs: when observations are correlated within clusters (e.g., students within schools, panel data), use CLUSTERED SANDWICH: replace the "meat" sum_{i} x_i x_i^T e_i^2 with sum_{c} (sum_{i in c} x_i e_i) (sum_{i in c} x_i e_i)^T (sum over clusters c of the cluster-level outer products) — accounts for arbitrary within-cluster correlation. The number of clusters must be large (>= 50 typically) for asymptotic validity. HAC (HETEROSCEDASTICITY-AUTOCORRELATION-CONSISTENT) SEs (NEWEY-WEST): for time series with arbitrary autocorrelation up to lag L, use a kernel-weighted sum of autocovariances (Bartlett kernel) in the meat. PRACTICAL: in modern econometrics and social science, ROBUST SEs are the DEFAULT for OLS inference — they cost little efficiency under homoscedasticity and provide protection against heteroscedasticity. Always report them unless you are confident the GM assumptions hold (e.g., designed experiment with homoscedastic errors).',
  'ch08p9_robust_standard_errors_hc',
  'formula_recall',
  ['robust standard errors', 'Huber-White', 'sandwich', 'HC0', 'HC1', 'HC2', 'HC3', 'cluster-robust', 'Newey-West']
)

